.TH "JNEXT" "1" "" "jnext" "jnext Manual"
.SH NAME
jnext \- real\-time ZX Spectrum Next emulator with an integrated
debugger
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]jnext\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]] [\f[I]file\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]jnext\f[R] is a real\-time, cross\-platform ZX Spectrum Next
emulator written in C++17 and derived from the official VHDL sources of
the ZX Spectrum Next FPGA core.
It emulates the Next itself as well as the ZX Spectrum 48K, 128K and
+2A/+3 machines, and carries a debugger that shows CPU, memory, video
layers, sprites, audio and NextREG state live.
.PP
\f[I]file\f[R] is a program to load, exactly equivalent to
\f[B]\-\-load\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], so \f[CR]jnext game.tap\f[R] just
works.
Anything starting with \f[CR]\-\f[R] is treated as an option, never as a
filename.
Name the program one way or the other, not both at once:
\f[CR]jnext \-\-load game.tap game.tap\f[R] is rejected, and so is
naming two files.
.PP
The examples below use the Qt6 GUI build
(\f[CR]build/gui\-release/jnext\f[R]).
The SDL\-only build (\f[CR]build/sdl\-release/jnext\f[R]) takes the same
options minus the GUI and debugger ones.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Machine and program
.TP
\f[B]\-\-machine\f[R] \f[I]TYPE\f[R]
Machine type: \f[CR]48k\f[R], \f[CR]128k\f[R], \f[CR]plus3\f[R],
\f[CR]next\f[R] (default).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-load\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load a program.
The format is detected from the extension: \f[CR].nex\f[R],
\f[CR].jns\f[R], \f[CR].sna\f[R], \f[CR].szx\f[R], \f[CR].z80\f[R],
\f[CR].tap\f[R], \f[CR].tzx\f[R], \f[CR].wav\f[R], \f[CR].rzx\f[R].
(\f[CR].rzx\f[R] is accepted here and plays back, as
\f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R].)
.RS
.PP
\f[CR].jns\f[R] is jnext\(cqs own whole\-machine snapshot (see
\f[B]JNEXT SNAPSHOTS\f[R] below).
Loading one restores the machine it was taken on, including the machine
\f[I]type\f[R]: a \f[CR].jns\f[R] taken on a Next loads as a Next even
if \f[B]\-\-machine\f[R] says otherwise, the same way a \f[CR].sna\f[R],
\f[CR].szx\f[R] or \f[CR].z80\f[R] already does.
.PP
A NEX file with bytes after the banks its header declares is an
\f[I]extended\f[R] NEX.
If its header asks for the file to be kept open (any non\-zero
\f[CR]file_handle\f[R]: 1 to 0x3FFF hands the program the handle in
\f[CR]BC\f[R], 0x4000 and above writes it at that address), the program
can read the extra bytes from its own file.
If the header says \f[CR]file_handle\f[R] 0, jnext does what the
Next\(cqs own loader does: it loads only the declared banks, ignores the
trailing bytes and starts the program.
More than 16 KB of ignored bytes also logs a warning.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-nex\-args\f[R] \f[I]LINE\f[R]
Argument line for a NEX \f[B]V1.3\f[R] program.
\f[I]LINE\f[R] is placed, verbatim and zero\-terminated, in the CLI
buffer the file\(cqs header declares (its address and size).
When the program starts, \f[CR]DE\f[R] holds that address plus the size
\- one past the buffer \- which is where the reference V1.3 loader
leaves it (the V1.3 header comment says the address itself).
A line as long as or longer than that buffer is truncated to the
buffer\(cqs size with no terminator \- which is what the reference V1.3
loader does.
Quote \f[I]LINE\f[R] to pass more than one word:
\f[CR]jnext \-\-load game.nex \-\-nex\-args \(dqlevel 3\(dq\f[R].
Only V1.3 declares a CLI buffer, so the option is inert \- with a
warning \- for a \f[CR]V1.0\f[R]\-\f[CR]V1.2\f[R] file, or a V1.3 file
whose header declares no buffer.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-experimental\-nex\-v1.3\f[R]
Allow loading NEX \f[B]V1.3\f[R] files.
NEX V1.3 is an experimental format and is \f[B]not supported in any
way\f[R]; without this flag, loading a NEX file whose header version
exceeds \f[CR]V1.2\f[R] is refused with an error naming the version
found (exit status 1).
In the GUI, selecting such a file shows a warning dialog instead, with
Cancel as the default.
\f[CR]V1.0\f[R]\-\f[CR]V1.2\f[R] files are unaffected either way.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Mount SD\-card image \f[I]FILE\f[R] (\f[CR].img\f[R]).
Optional; see \f[B]SD CARD AND ROMS\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-confirm\f[R]
Skip the download prompt for the default\-location image and proceed.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-force\f[R]
Force re\-download and re\-patch of the default\-location image, to
recover a corrupted one.
Ignored when \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] is given: an explicit path always
wins.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-readonly\f[R]
Open the SD image read\-only, so the host file is never modified.
The emulated machine sees a write\-protected card: writes are rejected
with the SD write\-error token rather than silently discarded.
Use it when a run must not disturb an image other runs share.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-add\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Copy host \f[I]FILE\f[R] \(em a file, or a directory with everything in
it \(em into the SD\-card image and \f[B]exit without starting
emulation\f[R].
Without \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-dest\f[R] it lands in the root of the
card under its own name.
Give it once per run.
The image written is the one \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] names, or the
default\-location image when \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] is omitted \(em in
which case a loud warning says so, because that image is shared with
every other run.
See \f[B]PUTTING A FILE ON THE CARD\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-dest\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
Optional.
The path on the card that \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-add\f[R]\(cqs
\f[I]FILE\f[R] becomes, from the root of the card
(\f[CR]/NEXTZXOS/DRV\-A.DSK\f[R]).
The leading \f[CR]/\f[R] is optional, directories are separated with
\f[CR]/\f[R], and any missing directory in the path is created.
For a directory, \f[CR]/\f[R] copies its contents straight into the root
of the card.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-force\f[R]
Let \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-add\f[R] replace files that are already
there.
Without it an existing destination file is refused and nothing is
written.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-warm\-start\-regenerate\f[R]
Discard the cached warm\-start recording and take a fresh one on the
next \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] of a \f[CR].nex\f[R] file.
.RS
.PP
A \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] of a \f[CR].nex\f[R] on the ZX Spectrum Next runs
the program on a RECORDING of a real firmware boot, not on the machine
\f[B]jnext\f[R] assembles.
This is the behaviour, not an option: on the first load with a given SD
image \f[B]jnext\f[R] cold\-boots it (\f[CR]nextboot.rom\f[R] \->
\f[CR]TBBLUE.FW\f[R] \-> NextZXOS, a few seconds), stores the resulting
machine state under \f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/warm\-start/\f[R], and applies the
program on top of it \(em which is what \f[CR].nexload\f[R] does on real
hardware.
Later loads restore that state instead of booting.
If no recording can be made or used \(em no SD image, a card with no
firmware on it, a recording that will not load back \(em \f[B]jnext\f[R]
says so on standard error and falls back to the machine it has always
assembled.
.PP
The recording is keyed by the SD image\(cqs contents, the machine type
and the state format, so it invalidates itself whenever any of those
change.
Use this option to force the issue anyway, after replacing
\f[CR]TBBLUE.FW\f[R] or NextZXOS on the card.
It applies to \f[CR].nex\f[R] files on the Next only: the other machine
types run no firmware, and snapshots and tapes are loaded by mechanisms
a firmware\-resident machine does not have.
.PP
The recording is made on your machine from the image you mounted, and is
never shipped with \f[B]jnext\f[R]: it contains NextZXOS and DivMMC ROM
content.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-speed\f[R] \f[I]PERCENT\f[R]
Emulator throttle: 50 = half, 100 = normal, 200 = 2x, 400 = 4x.
Clamped to 10..1000.
Works in the GUI and the SDL\-only build; \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] runs
uncapped, so there it has no effect, and \f[B]jnext\f[R] warns.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-when\-slow\-prefer\f[R] \f[I]WHAT\f[R]
What to sacrifice on a host that cannot emulate in real time:
\f[CR]audio\f[R] (default) or \f[CR]video\f[R].
It changes nothing on a host with headroom.
.RS
.PP
\f[CR]audio\f[R] keeps the sound card fed by emulating two frames in one
frame slot when it runs low.
The first frame of such a pair is overwritten before it can be painted,
so the picture advances in an uneven, lurching way while the sound stays
clean.
.PP
\f[CR]video\f[R] never drops a frame for the sound card.
The extra frame runs in the next slot instead, so it gets its own
present, and the machine simply runs slower than real time.
The sound queue then runs down to its floor and the last sample level is
held to bridge the gap, which is heard as stutter and a dropped pitch.
This is how FUSE\-style emulators degrade.
.PP
Interactive frontends only; inert under \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R], which is
uncapped and has no audio device.
The GUI equivalent is \f[I]When the host is too slow\f[R] under
\f[B]Settings > Preferences > Startup\f[R], which applies live.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy1\-source\f[R] \f[I]SRC\f[R]
Host source for Joy 1 (port 0x1F): \f[CR]sdl\f[R] (autodetected gamepad,
default) or \f[CR]keys\f[R] (host arrow keys, Space = fire).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy2\-source\f[R] \f[I]SRC\f[R]
Host source for Joy 2 (port 0x37): \f[CR]sdl\f[R] (default) or
\f[CR]keys\f[R].
Only one connector may use \f[CR]keys\f[R].
Interactive (SDL/Qt) frontends only; inert under \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Attach \f[I]FILE\f[R] as a serial byte source on the joystick\-port UART
RX pin \(em the \(lqPC\(rq end of a joy\-port serial cable, as used by a
dezogif / DeZog serial rig.
.RS
.PP
The bytes do not reach the guest through either UART header.
They arrive through the NR 0x0B I/O\-mode multiplexer, which the guest
controls, so the guest hears them ONLY while all three of these hold:
.IP \(bu 2
bits 7 and 5 of NR 0x0B are both set (\f[CR]joy_iomode_uart_en\f[R]),
i.e.\ pin 7 is in one of the two UART modes.
Modes \f[CR]00\f[R] (static) and \f[CR]01\f[R] (CTC\-toggled) carry no
UART and receive nothing;
.IP \(bu 2
bit 4 selects the connector this cable is plugged into \(em see
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-connector\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
bit 0 picks the channel that receives it: 0 = UART 0, 1 = UART 1.
While the cable owns a channel, the module on that channel\(cqs header
(the ESP\-01 on UART 0, the Pi header on UART 1) can neither be heard
nor be spoken to, which is what the hardware does.
.PP
Delivery is paced at the receiving channel\(cqs current baud rate, so a
stream longer than the 512\-byte RX FIFO does not overrun a guest that
is draining it.
Bytes sent while the guest is not listening are LOST rather than queued,
exactly as on the wire \(em use
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\-delay\-frames\f[R] to schedule them.
If the whole stream is lost, jnext says so with a warning rather than
finishing quietly.
.PP
An unreadable or empty \f[I]FILE\f[R] is refused at startup.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\-delay\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Hold the \f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R] stream for \f[I]N\f[R] complete
frames before its first byte arrives (default 0, i.e.\ from the first
instruction).
This is how a byte is made to land while the program is already running
\(em the asynchronous\-break case \(em rather than being spent before
the program has set NR 0x0B up.
Requires \f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-connector\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Which joystick socket the joy\-port serial cable is in: \f[CR]1\f[R] or
\f[CR]2\f[R] (default \f[CR]2\f[R], the connector a real rig uses).
NR 0x0B bit 4 selects the connector the machine reads, so a guest that
selects the other one hears nothing at all.
Applies to \f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R], \f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-fifo\f[R]
and \f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-pty\f[R], and requires one of them.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-fifo\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]
Attach a LIVE, BIDIRECTIONAL serial cable on the joystick\-port UART, as
a pair of FIFOs named from \f[I]PATH\f[R]: \f[I]PATH\f[R]\f[CR].rx\f[R]
carries host to Next and \f[I]PATH\f[R]\f[CR].tx\f[R] carries Next to
host.
Both names are from the guest\(cqs point of view, the same way round as
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R].
Either is created if it does not exist; a path that exists and is not a
FIFO is refused.
.RS
.PP
This is the option to use for a debugger on the host \(em DeZog driving
dezogif_ng \(em because it carries a conversation.
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R] is a RECORDING: it reads its file whole at
startup, so pointing it at a FIFO waits for the writer to close and then
replays what it got.
The two are mutually exclusive: one cable, one socket.
.PP
The guest side is gated by NR 0x0B exactly as
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R] is, with one asymmetry that is the
hardware\(cqs: RECEIVE is connector\-selected (bit 4 picks one
socket\(cqs pin to listen on), TRANSMIT is not \(em the Next presents
joystick pin 7 to both sockets in turn, so a cable in the socket bit 4
does not select can still hear the machine while being unable to be
heard by it.
.PP
NOTHING BLOCKS.
With no peer attached, received bytes are simply absent and transmitted
bytes wait; a peer that attaches later gets what was queued.
A peer that goes away mid\-session ends the conversation: what was
queued is discarded rather than handed to whatever connects next, and
the cable re\-opens for a peer that reconnects.
Receive is paced at the receiving channel\(cqs current baud, so a large
host message cannot overrun the 512\-byte RX FIFO.
.PP
A rewind or an RZX playback holds the cable INERT \(em a re\-executed
frame neither re\-transmits (the peer cannot un\-receive a byte) nor
consumes host bytes the resumed timeline still needs.
Nothing about a live cable is saved in a snapshot.
Rewinding does not rewind the program on the other end of the cable, so
a debugger session will not survive one.
.PP
POSIX only.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-pty\f[R]
As \f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-fifo\f[R], but allocate a pseudo\-terminal
instead and log the slave device path at startup.
This is the form a serial client opens \(em DeZog\(cqs serial remote
wants one serial device, not a pair of pipes \(em and it is put in raw
mode so a binary protocol passes through untranslated.
POSIX only.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-tape\-realtime\f[R]
Real\-time tape loading, at the speed of an actual tape, instead of fast
load.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-tape\-save\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Append blocks SAVEd through the 48K ROM SA\-BYTES routine to
\f[I]FILE\f[R] (\f[CR].tap\f[R]).
Trap\-based: it fires when the ROM save routine at 0x04C2 runs with ROM
paged at slot 0.
Without this option no SAVE capture happens.
Cannot be combined with RZX recording or playback: the trap skips the
ROM routine, which a recording cannot replay.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R]
Answer a few \f[CR]RST $08\f[R] esxDOS calls for any program, for the
whole session: the version query, one in\-memory file and
\f[CR]run sibling.nex\f[R] chaining.
Every other call goes to the code at \f[CR]$0008\f[R].
For programs run without NextZXOS; a directly loaded NEX gets a fuller
set automatically (see \f[B]LOADING PROGRAMS\f[R]).
With NextZXOS booted it answers in front of NextZXOS\(cqs own esxDOS for
those calls, and NextZXOS\(cqs file commands stop working.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-root\f[R] \f[I]DIR\f[R]
Serve the host directory \f[I]DIR\f[R] to the guest through the esxDOS
file and directory calls, instead of the single in\-memory file.
Implies \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R].
Read\-only unless \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-writable\f[R] is also given.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Which programs see \f[BI]DIR\f[B], and which do not.\f[R] A NEX
loaded with \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] sees it, and so do dot commands: those
reach the filesystem through \f[CR]RST $08\f[R], which is what jnext
intercepts.
\f[B]NextZXOS does not see it\f[R] \(em not its Browser, not its file
selector, not BASIC\(cqs \f[CR]LOAD\f[R], not its loader.
NextZXOS carries its own SD\-card driver and its own FAT code in ROM and
reaches the card directly, without ever executing an \f[CR]RST $08\f[R],
so there is no point at which jnext could answer for it.
That boundary is permanent and deliberate; it is not a limitation that a
later version lifts.
\f[B]To put a file where NextZXOS can see it, copy it into the SD\-card
image.\f[R]
.PP
Inside \f[I]DIR\f[R], paths behave as FAT paths: \f[CR]/\f[R] and
\f[CR]\(rs\f[R] both separate components, a leading separator is the
root of \f[I]DIR\f[R] rather than the host\(cqs root, lookup ignores
case, and the drive letters \f[CR]*:\f[R], \f[CR]$:\f[R] and
\f[CR]c:\f[R] all mean \f[I]DIR\f[R].
A path that would leave \f[I]DIR\f[R], and any symbolic link, is
refused; symbolic links are not listed either.
Because \f[CR]\(rs\f[R] is a separator, a host file whose name contains
one cannot be reached \(em FAT cannot name such a file either.
Where two host files differ only in case and the guest\(cqs spelling
matches neither exactly, the first in byte order is taken.
.PP
Directory entries carry the host file\(cqs modification time.
\f[CR]M_GETDATE\f[R] answers from the emulated clock instead, so it
follows \f[B]\-\-rtc\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Do not combine it with a booted NextZXOS.\f[R] It implies
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] and inherits its limitation: the file calls
are then answered in front of NextZXOS\(cqs own esxDOS, and
NextZXOS\(cqs file commands stop working \(em \f[CR].ls\f[R] reports
\f[I]No such file or dir\f[R].
This option is for programs jnext loads itself.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-writable\f[R]
Allow the guest to create, truncate and write files under
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-root\f[R].
Off by default, and deliberately a separate flag: a guest write changes
a real file on the host immediately, and rewinding the emulator cannot
undo it.
Reads are fully rewindable; writes are not.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-rtc\f[R] \f[I]\(lqYYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM:SS\(rq\f[R]
Pin the RTC to a fixed date and time (a frozen clock) instead of
following the host clock, which makes boot screenshots deterministic.
The ISO \f[CR]YYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM:SS\f[R] form is also accepted.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-silent\f[R]
Disable all sound output (beeper, the three AY/YM chips, DAC/Covox/
Specdrum).
No audio device is opened and the emulator skips PSG and mixer sample
synthesis entirely, which can measurably speed up CPU\-bound runs.
Tape loading (EAR input) is unaffected.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load a raw binary into RAM.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\-org\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Load address for \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] (hex, default \f[CR]8000\f[R]).
Requires \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\-pc\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Entry point for \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] (hex, default: same as
\f[B]\-\-inject\-org\f[R]).
Requires \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-inject\-delay\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Wait \f[I]N\f[R] frames before injecting (default 0).
Use around 100 if the binary calls ROM routines that need the system
variables set up first.
Requires \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R].
.SS Networking (ESP\-01 WiFi)
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R]
Enable the emulated ESP\-01 WiFi module on UART 0.
\f[B]Off by default\f[R] \- see \f[B]NETWORKING\f[R] below for what
turning it on gives the running program.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-no\-esp\f[R]
Force the ESP off for this run, overriding a saved GUI preference that
enables it.
Given both, whichever comes last on the command line wins.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] \f[I]HOST\f[R]
Only let the running program connect to \f[I]HOST\f[R].
Repeatable: give the option once per host.
\f[I]HOST\f[R] is a single name \- a comma is \f[B]rejected\f[R] rather
than taken as a separator or as part of the name, because a name that
silently matched nothing would look like a restriction that is in force
when it is not.
Matching is exact and case\-insensitive, with no wildcards, and an IP
address must be listed as itself.
With no \f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] at all the program may name any host
(subject to the always\-refused addresses in \f[B]NETWORKING\f[R]).
Requires \f[B]\-\-esp\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-listen\-address\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Bind the running program\(cqs \f[CR]AT+CIPSERVER\f[R] to \f[I]ADDR\f[R],
default \f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R].
\f[I]ADDR\f[R] is a numeric IP address, never a name \- an address
resolved through DNS could change under you.
The default means a program that opens a server is reachable only from
this machine; widening it (\f[CR]0.0.0.0\f[R]) exposes that program to
your network, which is why it has to be asked for.
Nothing listens until the program itself sends \f[CR]AT+CIPSERVER\f[R].
Requires \f[B]\-\-esp\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-ip\-address\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Report \f[I]ADDR\f[R] as the module\(cqs own station address instead of
the default \f[CR]192.168.1.50\f[R], so a program that recognises its
own address can be run against the one it expects.
It is what \f[CR]AT+CIFSR\f[R] and \f[CR]AT+CIPSTA?\f[R] answer, and
nothing more: \f[B]nothing binds it, nothing routes through it\f[R], and
the emulated Next cannot be reached at it \- that is
\f[B]\-\-esp\-listen\-address\f[R], which is a real socket.
\f[I]ADDR\f[R] is a numeric IP address, never a name, and
\f[CR]0.0.0.0\f[R] is refused because that is what the module reports
while it has no network.
Requires \f[B]\-\-esp\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-ip\-address\-after\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Report \f[I]ADDR\f[R] instead, once the outage ends.
Defaults to the pre\-outage address, which is what a short outage really
does; give this when the address should come back \f[B]different\f[R] \-
a DHCP lease that expired during a long outage, or a router that
reassigned.
It is what makes a program\(cqs stale\-address handling testable at all:
across an outage that returns the \f[I]same\f[R] address, a program that
caches its address at start\-up and one that re\-reads it are
indistinguishable.
Requires \f[B]\-\-esp\-delayed\-associate\-frames\f[R], and takes the
same values as \f[B]\-\-esp\-ip\-address\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-delayed\-disassociate\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Take the emulated module off its WiFi network after \f[I]N\f[R] emulated
frames, as if the access point had gone away.
From then on \f[CR]AT+CIFSR\f[R] reports
\f[CR]+CIFSR:STAIP,\(dq0.0.0.0\(dq\f[R] instead of the module\(cqs
address, which is what a real ESP\-01 with no association reports and
what lets a program detect that its address has gone.
Nothing else changes: connections already open keep running, new ones
still open, and no unsolicited \f[CR]WIFI DISCONNECT\f[R] is sent \-
what a real module does to traffic during an outage has not been
measured, and jnext does not invent it.
Requires \f[B]\-\-esp\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-delayed\-associate\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Put it back on the network after \f[I]N\f[R] emulated frames, so
\f[CR]AT+CIFSR\f[R] reports the module\(cqs address again \- the same
address as before, since a short outage does not normally change it.
Requires \f[B]\-\-esp\-delayed\-disassociate\-frames\f[R], and
\f[I]N\f[R] must be greater than its value: an outage that ends before
it starts is refused rather than run.
.SS Recording and playback
.TP
\f[B]\-\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record video and audio to an MP4.
Requires \f[B]ffmpeg\f[R](1) on the PATH.
If the recording cannot be produced (encoder failure, no usable output),
\f[B]jnext\f[R] reports the error and exits non\-zero.
If the recording cannot even be started (no usable \f[B]ffmpeg\f[R]),
\f[B]jnext\f[R] exits non\-zero immediately, without running the
emulation.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-wav\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record the mixed stereo output to a 44.1 kHz, 16\-bit PCM WAV.
Works headless and requires neither an audio device nor ffmpeg.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-dac\-trace\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record \f[CR]segment,tstate,channel,value\f[R] rows for physical DAC
writes.
A cold boot starts a new segment.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-db\f[R] \f[I]DB\f[R]
Set master host audio gain from \-24 dB to +24 dB (default 0 dB).
The gain is applied after the emulated hardware mix; overflow saturates
at the 16\-bit PCM limits.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-beeper\-db\f[R] \f[I]DB\f[R]
Set host gain for beeper EAR/MIC/tape\-EAR audio from \-24 dB to +24 dB.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-ay0\-db\f[R] \f[I]DB\f[R]
Set host gain for TurboSound AY chip 0 from \-24 dB to +24 dB.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-ay1\-db\f[R] \f[I]DB\f[R]
Set host gain for TurboSound AY chip 1 from \-24 dB to +24 dB.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-ay2\-db\f[R] \f[I]DB\f[R]
Set host gain for TurboSound AY chip 2 from \-24 dB to +24 dB.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-dac\-db\f[R] \f[I]DB\f[R]
Set host gain for Specdrum, Soundrive and Covox DAC audio from \-24 dB
to +24 dB.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Play back an RZX recording from the start of the run.
Works the same in the GUI, the SDL\-only build and under
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
A recording that fails to load is logged, and \f[B]jnext\f[R] then exits
non\-zero.
The machine starts exactly as it does for \f[B]\-\-load\f[R]
\f[I]FILE\f[R], so the two spellings replay identically, and so do
\f[B]File > Load NEX File\&...\f[R] and \f[B]File > Play RZX
Recording\f[R] in the GUI.
The recording brings its own snapshot of the machine, so it cannot be
combined with \f[B]\-\-rzx\-record\f[R], with \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] of another program, or with a second RZX file.
It plays on the machine it was recorded on, whatever machine is
configured: a recording \f[B]jnext\f[R] makes names its machine, and one
from another emulator is judged by its embedded snapshot (an SNA\(cqs
size, an SZX\(cqs machine ID, a \f[CR].z80\f[R]\(cqs hardware mode).
A recording that names none \(em one \f[B]jnext\f[R] 1.0.0 or earlier
made with an SNA snapshot, or one whose snapshot is of a machine
\f[B]jnext\f[R] does not emulate \(em plays on the configured machine.
An explicit \f[B]\-\-machine\f[R] given with the recording wins, and
\f[B]jnext\f[R] warns when it disagrees with the recording, which then
goes out of step; a recording played from the GUI\(cqs menus always gets
its own machine.
The recording\(cqs machine stays selected afterwards, in the GUI\(cqs
\f[B]Machine > Machine Type\f[R] too, as if chosen there.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-rzx\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record input to an RZX file from the start of the run \(em or, with
\f[B]\-\-load\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R], from the moment that program
is in the machine, so the snapshot the file carries is the program, and
playback replays it.
The file is written when \f[B]jnext\f[R] exits (or, in the GUI, at
\f[B]File > Stop RZX Recording\f[R]).
Works the same in the GUI, the SDL\-only build and under
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
While recording, a tape loads in real time: a fast load skips the ROM
loader, which a recording cannot replay.
The snapshot is an SZX on the 128K and +3 and a 48K SNA on the 48K.
The file names the machine it was recorded on, which
\f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R] then uses.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Recording is not available on a ZX Spectrum Next, which is
jnext\(cqs default machine, so this needs an explicit \-\-machine 48k,
128k or plus3.\f[R] An RZX carries a snapshot of the machine it starts
from plus a log of the values the program read from ports \(em and the
log records the values without the ports they came from.
So no snapshot an RZX can carry both describes a Next (none of SNA, SZX
or Z80 holds its extra RAM, NextREGs, Layer 2, tilemap, sprites or
Copper) and keeps the value log aligned with what the restored machine
goes on to read.
jnext refuses rather than write a file that looks fine and replays
wrong.
\f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R] still plays a Next recording made by an earlier
jnext, with a warning that it may not replay faithfully.
A \f[I]FILE\f[R] that cannot be written is refused before the machine
starts, and a recording that cannot be saved when it is written is
logged; either way \f[B]jnext\f[R] exits non\-zero.
A reset ends the recording: a hard reset (the Reset button, F1, or the
program\(cqs own), loading another program from the GUI, changing the
machine type, or F4 writes the file there, and nothing after it is
recorded \(em a recording cannot replay a reset.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-rewind\-buffer\-size\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Frame\-snapshot ring buffer for backwards execution.
Opt\-in; default 0 = off.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-trace\f[R]
Enable the per\-instruction trace log (a 10K\-entry ring).
Also enabled implicitly by \f[B]\-\-rewind\-buffer\-size\f[R]
\f[I]N\f[R] with \f[I]N\f[R] > 0, and toggleable in the debugger.
.SS Headless and automation
.TP
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R]
Run with no display and no audio device, at maximum speed.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-benchmark\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Headless only.
Run exactly \f[I]N\f[R] frames uncapped, print one machine\-parseable
\f[CR]BENCH\f[R] line (wall seconds, fps, T\-states/s, T\-states/frame,
CPU speed, host core, build type) plus a human summary to stdout, then
exit.
Used by \f[CR]make bench\f[R] (\f[CR]test/bench/bench.sh\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-benchmark\-label\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Workload label printed verbatim in the \f[CR]BENCH\f[R] line (default:
the loaded file\(cqs basename, or \f[CR]boot\-<machine>\f[R]).
No whitespace, since the \f[CR]BENCH\f[R] line is space\-delimited.
Requires \f[B]\-\-benchmark\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Save a screenshot after a delay.
The format is taken from \f[I]FILE\f[R]\(cqs extension: \f[CR].scr\f[R]
writes the raw ULA screen memory, anything else writes a PNG of the
composited picture.
See \f[B]SCREENSHOT FORMATS\f[R] below.
The capture is taken from the next frame the machine renders, so if it
comes due while the machine is paused \- by the debugger, or by a
\f[B]\-\-magic\-breakpoint\f[R] \- it waits until the machine runs
again; it is never written from the frame that was on screen before the
pause.
One still waiting when the exit comes is an error (see
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-time\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Delay in seconds (default 10).
Requires \f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Delay in frames.
Overrides \f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-time\f[R].
Requires \f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-layers\f[R] \f[I]LIST\f[R]
Layers to compose into the screenshot: a comma\-separated list of
\f[CR]ula\f[R], \f[CR]layer2\f[R], \f[CR]sprites\f[R], \f[CR]tiles\f[R],
\f[CR]all\f[R] (default \f[CR]all\f[R]).
Requires \f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\f[R], and is rejected when that
screenshot is a \f[CR].scr\f[R], which has no layers to choose from.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Exit the emulator after \f[I]N\f[R] seconds.
The exit always fires, but work the command line deferred to a later
frame and that has not happened by then is an error, and \f[B]jnext\f[R]
exits non\-zero: a \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] still waiting out its boot delay
(100 frames for \f[CR].tzx\f[R] and \f[CR].wav\f[R]), an
\f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] with \f[B]\-\-inject\-delay\f[R], an
\f[B]\-\-rzx\-record\f[R] waiting for that load, a
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\f[R], \f[B]\-\-delayed\-nmi\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\f[R] still to come, a
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\f[R] stream still held by
\f[B]\-\-joy\-uart\-rx\-delay\-frames\f[R], and an edge of the
\f[B]\-\-esp\-delayed\-disassociate\-frames\f[R] /
\f[B]\-\-esp\-delayed\-associate\-frames\f[R] outage not yet reached.
Give the exit headroom over the last of them.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Exit after \f[I]N\f[R] frames.
Overrides \f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R], and keeps its rule on
work still to come.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Headless only: requires \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
Save a snapshot after a delay in frames.
The format is chosen by the extension of \f[I]FILE\f[R]:
\f[CR].jns\f[R], \f[CR].szx\f[R], \f[CR].nex\f[R], anything else
\f[CR].sna\f[R].
A snapshot is only ever taken at a frame boundary, so if the debugger
has paused the machine part\-way through a frame \(em a magic
breakpoint, say \(em the frame in flight is completed first and the
snapshot is written from the boundary that follows it.
That pause never refuses the capture; the saved machine is then up to
one frame past the point the debugger stopped at.
A format that cannot represent the current machine \f[I]is\f[R] refused
\(em it writes nothing, says why, and exits non\-zero (see \f[B]JNEXT
SNAPSHOTS\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Delay in frames for \f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\f[R] (default 0).
Requires \f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-snapshot\-compression\f[R] \f[I]STATE\f[R]
Whether \f[CR].jns\f[R] snapshots are written deflated.
\f[CR]on\f[R] (the default) deflates every member; \f[CR]off\f[R] stores
them instead, so the file is readable with \f[CR]unzip \-p\f[R] and a
hex editor at roughly five times the size.
A debugging aid; it changes nothing about what the file contains, and
jnext reads both forms.
Any other \f[I]STATE\f[R] is an error.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-snapshot\-mode\f[R] \f[I]MODE\f[R]
How much a \f[CR].jns\f[R] load refuses.
One setting with three positions, because they are one axis and asking
for two of them at once is not a thing to want.
.RS
.PP
\f[CR]normal\f[R] (the default) warns about a mismatched state model
revision or mismatched ROM digests and refuses a different SD card.
.PP
\f[CR]strict\f[R] turns those provenance warnings into refusals: a
snapshot written by a jnext whose state model this build does not know,
or one whose recorded ROM digests differ from the ROMs now loaded.
A missing tape file is \f[B]not\f[R] covered \(em that always warns and
never refuses, because a machine restores perfectly well without the
tape it was loading from.
.PP
\f[CR]force\f[R] restores even though the mounted SD card is not the one
the snapshot was taken on, printing a warning that names both cards.
This is the setting that lets you create exactly the silently\-wrong
case the identity check exists to prevent, which is why it is spelled
out in full.
.PP
Any other \f[I]MODE\f[R] is an error.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\f[R] \f[I]SECS\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R]
Press \f[I]KEY\f[R] after \f[I]SECS\f[R] seconds.
Headless only (requires \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R]), repeatable.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R]
Press \f[I]KEY\f[R] after \f[I]N\f[R] emulated frames.
This is the frames\-unit spelling of \f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\f[R],
not an override of it: both forms queue into the same list, so giving
both schedules two keypresses.
Requires \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-nmi\f[R] \f[I]SECS\f[R] \f[I]BUTTON\f[R]
Press an NMI \f[I]BUTTON\f[R] after \f[I]SECS\f[R] seconds.
Headless only (requires \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R]), repeatable.
\f[I]BUTTON\f[R] is case\-insensitive and names which button to press,
spelled as the label on a real Next\(cqs case.
Of its three buttons, two raise an NMI: \f[CR]nmi\f[R] (aliases
\f[CR]mf\f[R], \f[CR]m1\f[R]) is the \f[B]NMI\f[R] button, wired to the
Multiface; \f[CR]drive\f[R] (alias \f[CR]divmmc\f[R]) is the
\f[B]DRIVE\f[R] button, wired to the DivMMC.
\f[B]RESET\f[R] is not an NMI button and is not accepted here.
The press goes through the same path as the host F9 / F10 hotkeys, so it
is subject to the same enable gates \(em NextREG 0x06 bit 3 for the
Multiface, bit 4 plus NextREG 0x83 bit 0 for the DivMMC \(em and a press
with its gate closed does nothing, exactly as on hardware.
One press generates one NMI, not a repeating one.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-nmi\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] \f[I]BUTTON\f[R]
Press \f[I]BUTTON\f[R] after \f[I]N\f[R] emulated frames.
This is the frames\-unit spelling of \f[B]\-\-delayed\-nmi\f[R], not an
override of it: both forms queue into the same list, so giving both
schedules two presses.
Requires \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-sdcard\-insert\-frames\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Insert SD\-card image \f[I]FILE\f[R] after \f[I]N\f[R] emulated frames,
exactly as \f[B]File > Insert SD Card Image\f[R] does (see \f[B]SD CARD
AND ROMS\f[R]).
The card keeps \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-readonly\f[R].
The run exits non\-zero if the insert is refused \- while an RZX
recording is made or played, while a directly loaded NEX keeps its own
file open, when \f[I]FILE\f[R] is not a readable file, or when another
insert is due in the same frame \- or if the automatic exit comes first.
An empty \f[I]FILE\f[R] is rejected.
Repeatable.
Requires \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-compositor\-trace\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Dump a per\-pixel compositor trace (CSV) for one frame.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-compositor\-trace\-frame\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Target frame for \f[B]\-\-compositor\-trace\f[R] (default 250).
Requires \f[B]\-\-compositor\-trace\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]KEY\f[R] is case\-insensitive and is one of: a single character
(\f[CR]a\f[R]\-\f[CR]z\f[R], \f[CR]0\f[R]\-\f[CR]9\f[R], \f[CR].\f[R],
\f[CR],\f[R], \f[CR];\f[R], \f[CR]:\f[R]), one of the symbolic names
\f[CR]ENTER\f[R], \f[CR]RETURN\f[R], \f[CR]SPACE\f[R], \f[CR]UP\f[R],
\f[CR]DOWN\f[R], \f[CR]LEFT\f[R], \f[CR]RIGHT\f[R], or a compound
\f[CR]sym+\f[R]\f[I]char\f[R] / \f[CR]caps+\f[R]\f[I]char\f[R] (for
example \f[CR]sym+m\f[R] is \f[CR].\f[R]).
.SS Debugging
.TP
\f[B]\-\-magic\-breakpoint\f[R]
Enable the magic\-breakpoint opcodes (\f[CR]ED FF\f[R] and
\f[CR]DD 01\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-persistent\-breakpoints\f[R]
Keep breakpoints and watchpoints armed while the debugger window is
\f[B]closed\f[R].
Without this option \(em the default \(em closing the window disarms
them, and nothing stops.
With it, a hit pauses the machine and reopens the debugger window at the
breakpoint.
With the window already open, behaviour is unchanged.
The breakpoint check then runs on every instruction for the whole run,
which is what \(lqpersistent\(rq costs; it is accepted but has no effect
in \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R], in the SDL\-only build and in builds without
the debugger, since only the debugger can set a breakpoint.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-dzrp\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R]
Serve the DeZog Remote Protocol (DZRP) on TCP \f[I]PORT\f[R], so DeZog
\- or any other DZRP client \- can debug the running machine; see
\f[B]REMOTE DEBUGGING (DEZOG)\f[R].
Off unless given.
\f[I]PORT\f[R] \f[CR]0\f[R] binds a free port the system chooses, and
the log says which (\f[CR]dzrp: listening on 127.0.0.1:40123\f[R]).
One client at a time: a second connection is closed at once.
A port that cannot be bound (already in use, say) is a startup error.
Works in every frontend, \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] included.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-gdb\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R]
Serve the GDB remote serial protocol on TCP \f[I]PORT\f[R], so
\f[CR]z88dk\-gdb\f[R] can debug the running machine; see \f[B]REMOTE
DEBUGGING (Z88DK\-GDB)\f[R].
Off unless given.
\f[I]PORT\f[R] \f[CR]0\f[R] binds a free port the system chooses, and
the log says which (\f[CR]gdb: listening on 127.0.0.1:40125\f[R]).
One client at a time.
A port that cannot be bound is a startup error.
May be given together with \f[B]\-\-dzrp\-port\f[R]: each server has its
own port and its own client.
Works in every frontend, \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] included.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-zrcp\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R]
Serve the ZEsarUX remote command protocol (ZRCP) on TCP \f[I]PORT\f[R],
so DeZog (its \f[CR]zrcp\f[R] remote) \- or \f[CR]telnet\f[R], or any
other ZRCP client \- can debug the running machine; see \f[B]REMOTE
DEBUGGING (ZRCP)\f[R].
Off unless given; \f[CR]10000\f[R] is the port those clients assume.
\f[I]PORT\f[R] \f[CR]0\f[R] binds a free port the system chooses, and
the log says which (\f[CR]zrcp: listening on 127.0.0.1:40123\f[R]).
One client at a time: a second connection is told so and closed.
A port that cannot be bound is a startup error.
Works in every frontend, \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] included, and alongside
\f[B]\-\-dzrp\-port\f[R] and \f[B]\-\-gdb\-port\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-debug\-listen\-address\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R]
Bind address for the debugger protocol servers
(\f[B]\-\-dzrp\-port\f[R], \f[B]\-\-gdb\-port\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-zrcp\-port\f[R]), default \f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R].
\f[I]ADDR\f[R] is a numeric IP address, never a name \- an address
resolved through DNS could change under you \- and anything else is
refused at startup.
The default means only this machine can reach the debugger; a
non\-loopback address (\f[CR]0.0.0.0\f[R]) exposes it to your network,
and none of the debugger protocols has any authentication.
Refused unless a server port is given too: an address for servers that
are all off would configure nothing.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-script\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load a debugger script (\f[CR].jds\f[R]); see \f[B]SCRIPTING\f[R].
Repeatable: the scripts load, and their rules run, in the order given.
A script that cannot be read, or that has any error, is reported as
\f[I]file\f[R]:\f[I]line\f[R]:\f[I]column\f[R]: \f[I]message\f[R] and
jnext exits 1 before the machine runs \- nothing of it runs partially.
In \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] and the SDL\-only build a script decides the
exit status: \f[CR]exit\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] exits \f[I]N\f[R], a
\f[CR]stop\f[R] or a failed \f[CR]assert\f[R] exits 3, a run\-time error
exits 1.
In the Qt GUI a script never ends the program: \f[CR]stop\f[R] and
\f[CR]exit\f[R] pause the machine instead.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-script\-key\f[R] \f[I]FRAME\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Deliver script host key \f[I]N\f[R] (\f[CR]1\f[R] to \f[CR]8\f[R]) at
emulated frame \f[I]FRAME\f[R] (\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] only;
repeatable): the script\(cqs \f[CR]on hostkey\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] rules run
at the end of frame \f[I]FRAME\f[R], where \f[CR]on frame\f[R]
\f[I]FRAME\f[R] runs.
Needs a \f[B]\-\-script\f[R], or a \f[B]\-\-record\-script\f[R] (key
\f[CR]8\f[R] is the recorder\(cqs capture).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-record\-script\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Record the session as a replay script; see \f[B]Recording a session\f[R]
under \f[B]SCRIPTING\f[R].
Every input change is written as the frame it landed on, each Alt + 8
(or \f[B]\-\-script\-key\f[R] \f[I]FRAME\f[R] \f[CR]8\f[R]) captures the
screen, and \f[I]FILE\f[R] is written when jnext exits.
Works in every frontend.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-map\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load a z88dk \f[CR].map\f[R] symbol table, so a script can name an
address as \f[CR]\(atsymbol\f[R], and the debugger shows the names (the
same table \f[B]Map > Load MAP\f[R] fills).
A file that cannot be loaded, or holds no symbols, is a startup error.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-magic\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R]
Enable the magic debug port at \f[I]PORT\f[R] (hex, for example
\f[CR]0x00FF\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-magic\-port\-mode\f[R] \f[I]MODE\f[R]
Magic\-port output mode: \f[CR]hex\f[R] (default), \f[CR]dec\f[R],
\f[CR]ascii\f[R], \f[CR]line\f[R].
Requires \f[B]\-\-magic\-port\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-profile\f[R]
Enable the CPU T\-state profiler.
It allocates an mmap\(cqd histogram and accumulates one entry per
executed instruction; on exit the histogram is written to
\f[B]\-\-profile\-output\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-profile\-output\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Output path for \f[B]\-\-profile\f[R] (default \f[CR]profile.dat\f[R]).
Requires \f[B]\-\-profile\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-log\-level\f[R] \f[I]SPEC\f[R]
Set per\-subsystem log levels; see \f[B]LOGGING\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-log\-file\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Write the log to \f[I]FILE\f[R] instead of the console.
\f[I]FILE\f[R] is truncated at the start of every run, and jnext exits
non\-zero if it cannot be opened rather than quietly logging to the
console instead.
See \f[B]LOGGING\f[R].
.SS Misc
.TP
\f[B]\-\-help\f[R], \f[B]\-h\f[R]
Print the built\-in help and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R], \f[B]\-V\f[R]
Print the version and exit.
.SH MACHINES
.TP
\f[CR]48k\f[R]
ZX Spectrum 48K \- the original rubber\-key Spectrum.
.TP
\f[CR]128k\f[R]
ZX Spectrum 128K \- AY sound and memory paging.
.TP
\f[CR]plus3\f[R]
ZX Spectrum +2A/+3 \- extended paging.
.TP
\f[CR]next\f[R]
ZX Spectrum Next (Issue 2) \- full Next hardware and extended features.
.PP
The machine type can also be switched at runtime from the GUI
(\f[B]Machine > Machine Type\f[R]); switching resets the machine.
.SH SD CARD AND ROMS
jnext takes its ROMs from an SD\-card image, exactly like real ZX
Spectrum Next hardware.
Where each one comes from:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]The FPGA boot ROM\f[R] (\f[CR]nextboot.rom\f[R], 8 KB) is
\f[I]silicon\-baked\f[R]: embedded in the jnext binary at link time.
No flag, no lookup; it mirrors the on\-FPGA flash IPL of the real
machine.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]The ROMs jnext loads itself\f[R] \- 48K / 128K / +3 BASIC, DivMMC
firmware, Multiface firmware and the Alt ROM \- are read from the
SD\-card image at the canonical TBBlue paths under
\f[CR]/MACHINES/NEXT/\f[R] (\f[CR]48.rom\f[R], \f[CR]128.rom\f[R],
\f[CR]plus3.rom\f[R], \f[CR]enNxtmmc.rom\f[R], \f[CR]enNextMf.rom\f[R],
\f[CR]enAltZX.rom\f[R]).
That is the whole list of files jnext reads from the card itself.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]NextZXOS is not one of them.\f[R] It is loaded by the firmware, not
by jnext: the machine boots \f[CR]/TBBLUE.FW\f[R] from the card through
the emulated SD interface and that firmware streams
\f[CR]/MACHINES/NEXT/enNextZX.rom\f[R] into memory itself, exactly as on
real hardware.
.PP
So even a \f[B]\-\-machine\f[R] \f[CR]48k\f[R] run needs an SD\-card
image: that is where \f[CR]48.rom\f[R] lives.
.PP
Point at an image with \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], or give no
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] at all: jnext then falls back to
\f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/sdcard/cspect\-next\-1gb\-fixed.img\f[R] (a
FAT32\-patched image), offering to download the canonical distribution
image (kept alongside it as \f[CR]cspect\-next\-1gb.img\f[R]) and
produce that patched copy on first run.
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-confirm\f[R] skips the prompt, which is
useful in scripts; \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-force\f[R] re\-downloads
and re\-patches a corrupted cached image.
An explicit \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] always wins over both.
.PP
Note that jnext opens the image \f[B]read\-write\f[R] by default, and
writes the emulated machine makes to the SD card are persisted to the
file.
Two runs sharing one image are therefore not independent: a run that
boots NextZXOS mutates the image the next run reads.
For a reproducible run, either pass \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-readonly\f[R], or
give it a private copy (\f[CR]cp \-\-reflink=auto\f[R] is instant and
costs no space on a copy\-on\-write filesystem).
.PP
The mounted image is also the SD card the emulated machine sees at
runtime, through the SPI/DivMMC path: NextZXOS reads its files from it.
.SS CHANGING THE CARD WHILE RUNNING
\f[B]File > Insert SD Card Image\&...\f[R] swaps the card in the running
machine, and \f[B]File > Eject SD Card\f[R] takes it out; nothing is
reset.
The Next has no card\-detect line, so the guest is not told: use
NextZXOS\(cqs \f[B]REMOUNT\f[R], which asks you to \f[I]Remove/insert SD
and press Y\f[R] \- change the card, then press Y.
.IP \(bu 2
The ROMs are not re\-read: they were loaded at power\-on, as on
hardware.
A later hard reset, or loading a program, reads them from the inserted
card.
After an eject, those put the last inserted card back.
.IP \(bu 2
Refused while an RZX recording is made or played, and while a directly
loaded NEX keeps its own file open.
The rewind history is discarded.
.IP \(bu 2
A \f[CR].jns\f[R] snapshot records the card it was taken on.
Once a \f[I]different\f[R] card is in (another FAT32 volume, partition
table or size; a copy of the same card is the same card) it refuses to
load: insert the original card again, or pass
\f[B]\-\-snapshot\-mode\f[R] \f[CR]force\f[R].
.SS PUTTING A FILE ON THE CARD
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-add\f[R] copies a host file, or a whole
directory, into the image and exits.
No emulator starts, nothing boots; it is a file\-copy command that
happens to live in the emulator binary, so getting programs onto the
card needs no \f[CR]mtools\f[R] and no loopback mount.
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-sdcard\-file\-add game.dsk \-\-sdcard\-file\-dest /NEXTZXOS/DRV\-A.DSK
jnext \-\-sdcard\-file\-add demo.nex \-\-sdcard\-file\-dest /DEMOS/demo.nex
jnext \-\-sdcard\-file\-add demo.nex
jnext \-\-sdcard\-file\-add mygames
jnext \-\-sdcard\-file\-add mygames \-\-sdcard\-file\-dest /GAMES/MINE
.EE
.PP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-dest\f[R] is the path on the card that the source
\f[I]becomes\f[R].
It is optional: without it the source goes into the root of the card
under its own name, so the third example writes \f[CR]/demo.nex\f[R] and
the fourth \f[CR]/mygames\f[R].
A directory given with \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-dest\f[R] \f[CR]/\f[R] has
its contents copied straight into the root instead.
Missing directories along the path are created, so the second example
works on a card with no \f[CR]/DEMOS\f[R].
Names may be long: an 8.3\-clean uppercase name such as
\f[CR]DRV\-A.DSK\f[R] is written as a plain short entry, and anything
else gets VFAT long\-name entries plus a generated short name, exactly
as any other FAT32 driver would write it.
.PP
One run copies one source, so the pairing is never in doubt: giving
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-add\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-dest\f[R]
twice is refused (exit 1), and the two may come in either order.
To copy several things at once, put them in a directory and add that.
.PP
A destination file that already exists is \f[B]refused\f[R], and the
file on the card is left alone \(em overwriting \f[CR]DRV\-A.DSK\f[R]
would destroy a disk image.
Pass \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-force\f[R] to replace it deliberately.
.PP
A directory is copied recursively, in name order:
.IP \(bu 2
A directory that already exists on the card is \f[B]merged into\f[R],
never replaced; the files in it follow the rule above, each one.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Symbolic links are followed\f[R]: FAT has no links, so the card
gets what a link points at.
A link that leads back into its own parent directories is refused, as is
a link to nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Empty directories\f[R] are created.
.IP \(bu 2
Refused, before anything is written: a device, FIFO or socket; a file or
directory that cannot be read; a file of 4 GiB or more; a name FAT
cannot hold (non\-ASCII, or one of \f[CR]\(dq * : < > ? \(rs |\f[R], or
ending in a dot or a space); and two names in one directory that differ
only in case, which FAT would make one file.
Nothing is renamed or silently skipped \(em hidden files included.
.PP
A file or a directory, the copy is \f[B]all or nothing\f[R].
Every refusal is decided before the first byte is written, so a refused
copy \(em including one that does not fit, counting the directories it
would have to make \(em leaves the card exactly as it was.
If the copy fails part\-way \(em the card fills up, or a file shrinks
while it is read \(em everything this run created, files and
directories, is removed again before jnext exits with an error.
Two things cannot be undone: a file \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-force\f[R]
had already replaced (the message says how many), and the extra cluster
an existing directory grew by to hold a new entry, since FAT never
shrinks a directory.
The free\-space check counts a file that
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-force\f[R] will replace at its full size, so a
forced copy onto a nearly full card can be refused even though it would
just fit.
.PP
The copy goes into the image \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] names.
With no \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] it goes into the default\-location image,
which every other run also boots and which the test suite resolves from;
jnext warns loudly when that happens.
It does not make a backup: these are gigabyte files, and
\f[CR]cp \-\-reflink=auto\f[R] is instant on a copy\-on\-write
filesystem.
.PP
The image must be a spec\-valid FAT32 (at least 65525 clusters) \(em the
same thing the Next\(cqs own firmware requires.
The image jnext provisions for itself already is one; a hand\-made
under\-clustered image is refused, and
\f[CR]tools/fix\-sdcard\-image.sh\f[R] re\-clusters one, applying the
same fixes jnext\(cqs own provisioning does (a default
\f[CR]config.ini\f[R], and the 24.11 distribution\(cqs 16 KB
\f[CR]zx81.rom\f[R] doubled so the firmware boot menu can load it).
.PP
The exit status says which of the things that can go wrong did; see
\f[B]EXIT STATUS\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]DRV\-A.DSK\f[R] is not an arbitrary example: NextZXOS auto\-mounts
any \f[CR].DSK\f[R] or \f[CR].P3D\f[R] found at
\f[CR]/NEXTZXOS/DRV\-\f[R]\f[I]X\f[R]\f[CR].DSK\f[R] (and
\f[CR]CPM\-\f[R]\f[I]X\f[R]\f[CR].\f[R]\f[I]ext\f[R]) as drive
\f[I]X\f[R], so copying one there is all it takes to have a +3 disk
available at the next boot.
.SH LOADING PROGRAMS
Use \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], or simply a bare filename
(\f[CR]jnext game.tap\f[R]).
The two are equivalent, so use one or the other: combining them
(\f[CR]jnext \-\-load game.tap game.tap\f[R]) is rejected, as is naming
two files.
The format is detected from the extension:
.TP
\f[CR].nex\f[R]
v1.0/1.1/1.2; pages, Layer 2 screen and palette from the header.
V1.3 is experimental and unsupported \- it loads only with
\f[B]\-\-experimental\-nex\-v1.3\f[R] (or the GUI warning\(cqs Proceed).
.TP
\f[CR].jns\f[R]
jnext\(cqs own whole\-machine snapshot, including the machine type \-
see \f[B]JNEXT SNAPSHOTS\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR].sna\f[R]
48K and 128K snapshots.
.TP
\f[CR].szx\f[R]
Chunked snapshots, zlib\-compressed pages.
.TP
\f[CR].z80\f[R]
v1/v2/v3, 48K and 128K, RLE\-compressed or raw pages.
.TP
\f[CR].tap\f[R]
Fast load via a ROM trap; \f[CR]LOAD \(dq\(dq\f[R] is auto\-typed.
.TP
\f[CR].tzx\f[R]
Fast load or real\-time.
The tape player plays the standard, turbo, tone, pulse, pure\-data and
direct\-recording blocks; a valid block it cannot play (CSW, generalised
data, the deprecated C64, emulation\-info and snapshot blocks, or an ID
a later TZX revision adds) is skipped with a warning, and the blocks
after it still load.
A file that is not a well\-formed TZX is refused.
.TP
\f[CR].wav\f[R]
RIFF/PCM EAR\-bit playback (8/16\-bit, mono/stereo).
.TP
\f[CR].rzx\f[R]
Played back as if \f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R] had been given.
.PP
\f[CR].tap\f[R] and \f[CR].tzx\f[R] load in real time instead with
\f[B]\-\-tape\-realtime\f[R].
.PP
RZX recording uses \f[B]\-\-rzx\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R].
The GUI\(cqs \f[B]File > Load NEX File\&...\f[R] dialog accepts all of
the above.
.PP
Raw binaries go straight into RAM with \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R]
\f[I]FILE\f[R] (\f[B]\-\-inject\-org\f[R] load address,
\f[B]\-\-inject\-pc\f[R] entry point, \f[B]\-\-inject\-delay\f[R] a
frame delay before injecting).
.SS esxDOS calls from a directly loaded NEX
On a real Next a NEX file is always started by NextZXOS, so the program
can use NextZXOS\(cqs esxDOS services (an \f[CR]RST $08\f[R] followed by
a call number).
A NEX loaded directly by jnext (\f[B]\-\-load\f[R], a bare file name, or
\f[B]File > Load NEX File\&...\f[R]) is not started by NextZXOS, so
jnext answers those calls itself \- ahead of the NextZXOS a warm start
leaves resident (\f[B]\-\-warm\-start\-regenerate\f[R]), exactly as
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] does with NextZXOS booted.
This starts when the NEX is loaded and stops at the next reset or soft
reset.
It answers:
.IP \(bu 2
the drive query (\f[CR]M_GETSETDRV\f[R]): the only drive is
\f[CR]C:\f[R], and asking for any other drive fails;
.IP \(bu 2
the version query (\f[CR]M_DOSVERSION\f[R]): NextZXOS 1.94, or 2.02 for
a NEX that keeps its own file open (below);
.IP \(bu 2
one file kept in memory: the program can create it, write it, and read
it back under the same name, which is enough for a high\-score or
settings file.
It is never written to disk and is gone when jnext exits;
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]run NAME.nex\f[R] (\f[CR]M_EXECCMD\f[R]), which loads another NEX
file from the same directory;
.IP \(bu 2
for a NEX whose header asks for its file to stay open: reading that
file, including data appended after its banks (also through the
block\-streaming calls), and reading, never writing, other files in the
same directory by name.
Such a NEX gets the host files instead of the in\-memory file (unless
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] is also given).
.PP
Every other call from \f[CR]$80\f[R] to \f[CR]$B1\f[R] returns an error
(carry set, \f[CR]A\f[R]=2), including calls NextZXOS does implement,
such as reading the current directory, listing a directory or reading
the date.
A program that needs one of those usually stops with its own error
message.
Codes above \f[CR]$B1\f[R] are not esxDOS calls and go to the code at
\f[CR]$0008\f[R].
.PP
Without \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-root\f[R] this is not a filesystem.
Apart from the kept\-open case above the program cannot see files on the
host or on the SD card, so a program that needs data files has to be
copied to the SD card with them and started from NextZXOS.
.SS A host directory for directly loaded programs
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-root\f[R] \f[I]DIR\f[R] replaces the single
in\-memory file with the real host directory \f[I]DIR\f[R].
\f[CR]F_OPEN\f[R], \f[CR]F_CLOSE\f[R], \f[CR]F_READ\f[R],
\f[CR]F_WRITE\f[R], \f[CR]F_SEEK\f[R], \f[CR]F_FGETPOS\f[R],
\f[CR]F_FSTAT\f[R], \f[CR]F_STAT\f[R] and \f[CR]F_SYNC\f[R] then work on
real files, and \f[CR]F_OPENDIR\f[R], \f[CR]F_READDIR\f[R],
\f[CR]F_TELLDIR\f[R], \f[CR]F_SEEKDIR\f[R], \f[CR]F_REWINDDIR\f[R],
\f[CR]F_GETCWD\f[R], \f[CR]F_CHDIR\f[R], \f[CR]F_GETFREE\f[R] and
\f[CR]M_GETDATE\f[R] work as well.
It is meant for developing a program that reads data files: edit them in
place and re\-run, instead of copying them into the SD\-card image each
time.
.PP
\f[B]It does not make the host directory the SD card.\f[R] Only programs
that reach the filesystem through \f[CR]RST $08\f[R] can see it \(em a
directly loaded NEX, and dot commands.
NextZXOS cannot: its Browser, file selector, BASIC and loader use its
own in\-ROM SD\-card driver and its own FAT code, and never execute an
\f[CR]RST $08\f[R] at all, so booting NextZXOS and looking for
\f[I]DIR\f[R] in the Browser will show nothing.
This was measured, not assumed, and it is a permanent boundary rather
than an unfinished feature.
Files that NextZXOS must see still go into the SD\-card image.
.PP
Some things it deliberately does not do.
Writes need the separate \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-writable\f[R] flag,
because a guest write is a real and irreversible change to a host file
that rewinding cannot take back.
Wildcard, sorted and filtered directory listings, and the
\f[CR]+3DOS\f[R] header modes, are refused rather than answered
approximately.
\f[CR]M_P3DOS\f[R], which lets a program call NextZXOS ROM routines
directly \(em including raw sector reads \(em is not answered either: a
host directory cannot supply sectors.
.PP
Paths are confined to \f[I]DIR\f[R].
A path that climbs out of it is refused, whichever separator it uses, a
guest absolute path such as \f[CR]/data/x.bin\f[R] means
\f[CR]DIR/data/x.bin\f[R] and never the host\(cqs own \f[CR]/data\f[R],
and symbolic links are refused rather than followed.
.PP
A \f[I]DIR\f[R] that does not exist, or is not a directory, is an error
at start\-up: jnext says so and exits non\-zero rather than running with
the option silently inert.
.PP
And it is not for use alongside a booted NextZXOS.
Implying \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] means it inherits the same
shadowing: with NextZXOS running, the file calls are answered before
NextZXOS\(cqs own esxDOS sees them, so its file commands break.
.PP
jnext answers only while ROM is paged in at \f[CR]$0000\f[R], as on a
real Next, where NextZXOS is reached through the DivMMC, which only
takes over from the ROM.
A program that pages its own RAM in at \f[CR]$0000\f[R] keeps its own
\f[CR]RST $08\f[R] code.
That rule also applies to \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] and to the
kept\-open file.
.PP
\f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] answers a smaller set for programs loaded any
other way (a snapshot, a tape, \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R]) and for the whole
session: the version query, the in\-memory file and
\f[CR]run NAME.nex\f[R] \(em or, with \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\-root\f[R],
the host directory described above.
Every other call goes to the code at \f[CR]$0008\f[R] as usual.
It is meant for programs run without NextZXOS: with NextZXOS booted, it
answers those calls in front of NextZXOS\(cqs own esxDOS, and
NextZXOS\(cqs file commands stop working.
.SH JNEXT SNAPSHOTS
A \f[CR].jns\f[R] file is jnext\(cqs own whole\-machine snapshot, and
the only format that can represent a ZX Spectrum Next: \f[CR].sna\f[R]
and \f[CR].szx\f[R] describe a 48K/128K/+3 machine, which a Next is not,
so asking for either on a Next is refused rather than written lossily.
.PP
The other formats are written for the machines they can describe, and
refused for the states they cannot.
A \f[CR].sna\f[R] follows the machine: a 48K gets the 48K form (49179
bytes), a 128K and an ordinary +3 get the 128K form (all eight RAM banks
and the 0xC000 paging register).
An SNA has no byte for the +3\(cqs second paging register, so a +3 in
\f[I]special paging\f[R], or with ROM 2 or ROM 3 paged, is refused with
\f[CR].szx\f[R] named as the format that can hold it.
An SNA names the bank at 0xC000 in a field that reaches only banks 0\-7,
so a machine using \f[B]extended paging\f[R] (port 0xDFFD, which works
on every machine type, not only a Next) is refused too, pointing at
\f[CR].jns\f[R] \(em \f[CR].szx\f[R] has no field for that register
either.
An SNA also carries no machine identifier, so FUSE reads a 128K one as a
Pentagon 128 \(em the right RAM and paging, a different timing model.
.PP
Write one with \f[B]File > Save Snapshot\&...\f[R] (Alt+Shift+S), or
headless with \f[B]\-\-delayed\-snapshot\f[R] naming a \f[CR].jns\f[R]
file.
Read one back with \f[B]\-\-load\f[R], a bare file name, or \f[B]File >
Load NEX File\&...\f[R], which lists \f[CR].jns\f[R] alongside every
other format.
On a Next the save dialog offers \f[CR].jns\f[R] first and appends it to
a name typed without an extension; on a 48K, 128K or +3 it offers
\f[CR].sna\f[R] first and appends that instead.
.PP
Loading a \f[CR].jns\f[R] restores the machine \f[I]type\f[R] as well as
its contents, so one taken on a Next comes back as a Next whatever
\f[B]\-\-machine\f[R] says.
A snapshot is only ever taken at a frame boundary: if the debugger has
stopped the machine part\-way through a frame, that frame is completed
first and the restored machine is up to one frame past where you
stopped.
.PP
The file does \f[B]not\f[R] contain the SD card \- a card image is a
gigabyte, and it holds NextZXOS.
It records the card\(cqs identity instead and checks it on load: a
different card is refused unless \f[B]\-\-snapshot\-mode\f[R]
\f[CR]force\f[R], while the same card changed since (which happens on
any run that writes to it) restores with a warning.
\f[B]\-\-snapshot\-mode\f[R] \f[CR]strict\f[R] additionally turns the
state\-model and ROM\-digest warnings into refusals, and
\f[B]\-\-snapshot\-compression\f[R] \f[CR]off\f[R] writes the members
stored rather than deflated, so the file can be read with
\f[CR]unzip \-p\f[R].
.PP
It does contain \f[B]all of RAM\f[R], which on a Next includes NextZXOS
and DivMMC ROM code paged into it.
Those are not redistributable: keep your snapshots rather than posting
them, and hand somebody a \f[CR].nex\f[R] instead.
.SH HEADLESS MODE, SCREENSHOTS AND RECORDING
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] runs the emulator with no window and no audio
device, as fast as the host allows: the mode built for scripting and CI.
.PP
Notes worth knowing:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-frames\f[R] is the deterministic one.
Frame counts are reproducible; wall\-clock seconds are not.
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames\f[R] is the same idea for the
exit bound, and wins over \f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] when
both are given.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\-\-rtc\f[R] makes boot screenshots reproducible by freezing the
clock, so the NextZXOS date and time on screen never change between
runs.
.IP \(bu 2
A screenshot that was asked for and never taken is an error.
If \f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] fires before the capture comes
due, or after it came due while the machine was paused (a
\f[B]\-\-magic\-breakpoint\f[R], say: nothing resumes a paused headless
machine, so no frame is rendered for it), jnext logs an error and exits
non\-zero instead of silently writing nothing or writing the picture
from before the pause.
The same holds for a \f[B]\-\-load\f[R], \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R] or
keypress still to come.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\-layers\f[R] isolates a layer.
An excluded layer is composed as if its hardware enable bit were clear,
so the remaining ones still follow the NR 0x15 priority order and the NR
0x4A fallback colour shows through where everything is transparent.
Excluding \f[CR]ula\f[R] also removes the border: the ULA is what draws
it.
.IP \(bu 2
Video recording (\f[B]\-\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R], or \f[B]File >
Record MPEG4 Video\f[R]) pipes video and audio to ffmpeg, which must be
installed.
.IP \(bu 2
WAV recording (\f[B]\-\-wav\-record\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]) captures the
mixed audio before the host playback device.
It works in headless mode and continues through cold boots such as
sibling\-NEX chaining.
It cannot be combined with \f[B]\-\-silent\f[R], which disables mixer
synthesis.
If the emulator exits abnormally, the file may retain its initial
zero\-length data header even though PCM follows it.
.IP \(bu 2
DAC tracing (\f[B]\-\-dac\-trace\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]) records guest DAC
writes before mixing.
Channels are numbered 0\-3 (A\-D) and timestamps are CPU T\-states.
.IP \(bu 2
Master and per\-subsystem host gains are available under \f[B]Settings >
Preferences > Audio\f[R], or for one run with the
\f[B]\-\-audio\-gain\-\f[R] family.
Beeper, each TurboSound AY chip and the DAC family can be balanced
independently before the master gain.
They change playback and recording volume without changing
guest\-visible audio state and survive cold boots.
Each explicit CLI value overrides its corresponding saved preference.
.SS SCREENSHOT FORMATS
The output format is never a separate option: it is whatever the
filename\(cqs extension asks for, in \f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\f[R],
in \f[B]File > Save Screenshot\&...\f[R] and in \f[B]File > Quick
Screenshot\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR].png\f[R]
The composited picture \(em every enabled layer, the border, the
palette, the ULA scroll \(em exactly as the window shows it.
The in\-memory frame is 640x256; the file is 640x512, each row written
twice, so the pixels are square and the image is 4:3 without
post\-processing.
.TP
\f[CR].scr\f[R]
The raw ULA screen memory, the format Spectrum graphics tools read and
write.
No composition, no palette, no scroll: it is a copy of the bytes the ULA
is fetching, from whichever screen bank is currently being displayed
(bank 5, or bank 7 while the 128K shadow screen is selected by port
0x7FFD bit 3).
.RS
.PP
\f[B]A \f[CB].SCR\f[B] can only describe the classic ULA layer.\f[R]
Layer 2, the tilemap, the sprites, the LoRes modes and the Next palettes
have no representation in it at all, and are not in the file \(em a
Layer 2 game captured to \f[CR].scr\f[R] yields whatever happens to be
sitting in the ULA screen memory, which is often blank.
Capture those as \f[CR].png\f[R].
.PP
Size depends on the Timex screen mode in port 0xFF bits 2:0:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(19.1n) lw(19.1n) lw(31.8n).
T{
Mode
T}@T{
Size
T}@T{
Contents
T}
_
T{
Standard
T}@T{
6912
T}@T{
6144 pixel bytes, then 768 attribute bytes
T}
T{
Standard, alt file (mode 1)
T}@T{
6912
T}@T{
the same, from the alternate screen at 0x6000/0x7800
T}
T{
Timex hi\-colour (modes 2, 3)
T}@T{
12288
T}@T{
pixel plane, then the per\-cell attribute plane
T}
T{
Timex hi\-res (modes 6, 7)
T}@T{
12288
T}@T{
the even pixel plane, then the odd one
T}
.TE
.PP
The 12288\-byte form is the de\-facto Timex \f[CR].SCR\f[R] layout, and
is exactly the two 6144\-byte planes the ULA fetches.
It does not record which of the two Timex modes produced it, nor the
hi\-res ink/paper colour held in port 0xFF bits 5:3, because the format
has nowhere to put either.
Writing 6912 bytes in those modes was rejected as worse: the final 768
bytes would be pixel data presented as attributes.
.PP
While the shadow screen is selected the ULA is forced back to the
standard mode by the hardware, so a shadow\-screen capture is always
6912 bytes.
.RE
.SH NETWORKING (ESP\-01 WiFi)
A real ZX Spectrum Next has a header for an ESP\-01 WiFi module on UART
0.
\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R] emulates one, so software written for it \- NXtel,
nextsync, \f[CR]newt\f[R], the NextZXOS \f[CR].HTTP\f[R] and
\f[CR].zxdb\-dl\f[R] dot commands \- can reach the network through your
host.
.PP
Both \f[B]TCP and UDP\f[R] are emulated.
UDP is what \f[CR]newt\f[R] uses to read the time from an internet SNTP
server, so \f[CR].newt sntp 0 pool.ntp.org\f[R] works; the optional
local\-port argument of \f[CR]AT+CIPSTART=\(dqUDP\(dq,...\f[R] is
honoured.
UDP \(lqmodes\(rq 1 and 2, which let the far end of a link become
whoever last sent a packet, are answered \f[CR]ERROR\f[R] rather than
accepted and ignored.
\f[B]Server mode\f[R] and \f[B]multiplexed connections\f[R] are emulated
too \- see \f[I]Letting a program listen\f[R] below.
TLS and transparent mode are not.
.SS It is off by default, and that is the point
An enabled ESP gives the running program an \f[B]outbound TCP and UDP
pipe out of the emulator\f[R], and the program can already read the
SD\-card image, which jnext opens read\-write.
A program you did not write is a program you are trusting when you turn
this on.
So jnext does not turn it on for you, and there is no way for a loaded
program to turn it on for itself.
.PP
When it \f[I]is\f[R] on, a windowed run shows an \f[B]ESP cell in the
status bar\f[R] for as long as the run lasts.
Its presence means the guest can reach the network; it names the host of
the most recent connection, and shows an allowlist refusal or a fault in
red.
There is no way to have the ESP enabled in a window without that cell
being there.
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] builds no window and so has no status bar: there
the \f[CR]esp01\f[R] log is the only report, which is why it reports
every connection at the default level.
.PP
An address the policy below refuses is reported as a \f[I]failure\f[R]
rather than an allowlist refusal, so it is not red; the reason
(\f[CR]address refused by policy: \&...\f[R]) is in the cell\(cqs
tooltip and in the log.
See \c
.UR https://github.com/jorgegv/jnext/issues/161
issue #161
.UE \c
\&.
.SS What is always refused, whatever you allow
These are refused by address, before any connection is attempted, and
\f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] cannot re\-enable them:
.IP \(bu 2
loopback \- \f[CR]127.0.0.0/8\f[R] and \f[CR]::1\f[R], i.e.\ daemons on
your own machine;
.IP \(bu 2
link\-local \- \f[CR]169.254.0.0/16\f[R] and \f[CR]fe80::/10\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
cloud metadata endpoints \- \f[CR]169.254.169.254\f[R],
\f[CR]100.100.100.200\f[R] and \f[CR]fd00:ec2::254\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
the unspecified addresses \f[CR]0.0.0.0/8\f[R] and \f[CR]::\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
multicast and reserved space \- \f[CR]224.0.0.0/4\f[R],
\f[CR]240.0.0.0/4\f[R], \f[CR]255.255.255.255\f[R], \f[CR]ff00::/8\f[R].
.PP
IPv6 spellings of an IPv4 address (\f[CR]::ffff:127.0.0.1\f[R], NAT64,
6to4) are resolved to what they actually reach and judged on that, so
they cannot be used to get around the list.
.PP
\f[B]Your own LAN is reachable.\f[R] RFC1918 addresses
(\f[CR]192.168.x.x\f[R], \f[CR]10.x.x.x\f[R],
\f[CR]172.16\-31.x.x\f[R]), carrier\-grade NAT
(\f[CR]100.64.0.0/10\f[R]) and IPv6 unique\-local (\f[CR]fc00::/7\f[R])
are deliberately \f[I]not\f[R] refused \- reaching a machine on your own
network is the main thing people use this for, and nextsync in
particular connects to a PC on the LAN.
The two cloud\-metadata addresses above sit inside those ranges and are
carved back out.
.SS Narrowing it further
\f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R] restricts the program to hosts you name:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-esp \-\-esp\-allow nx.nxtel.org NXtel.nex
.EE
.PP
Every connection to anything else is refused, the program gets the same
\f[CR]ERROR\f[R] it would get from a real module that could not connect,
and the refusal is reported in the log and in the status bar.
.SS Making it permanent
\f[B]Settings > Preferences > Network\f[R] holds the same two settings
for people who run one of these programs regularly: an enable checkbox
and the allowed\-host list, one host per line (a comma separates too,
matching the config file\(cqs own format).
Blank entries and repeats are dropped, and the list is greyed out while
the module is off, because it restricts nothing on its own.
.PP
The module is built when a machine starts, so this page cannot switch it
on or off underneath a running program: the change takes effect on the
next hard reset (\f[B]F1\f[R], or \f[B]Machine > Power Reset\f[R]) and
on the next launch.
The CLI still wins for a single run in both directions \-
\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R] over a saved \f[I]off\f[R], and \f[B]\-\-no\-esp\f[R]
over a saved \f[I]on\f[R].
.SS Letting a program listen (server mode)
A program can also \f[B]accept\f[R] incoming connections:
\f[CR]AT+CIPMUX=1\f[R] followed by \f[CR]AT+CIPSERVER=1,<port>\f[R]
makes jnext listen on that port and hand the program each connection
that arrives.
This is what a debug stub running on the emulated Next needs, because a
debugger on your PC dials out and never listens.
.PP
Everything above is about \f[I]outbound\f[R] connections \- which
addresses the program may dial.
A listening socket points the other way: it exposes the
\f[B]program\f[R] to whatever can reach the port, and the allowlist has
nothing to say about it, because the program does not choose who
connects.
So the boundary is the bind address instead:
.IP \(bu 2
by default the port is bound to \f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R], so only this
machine can reach it \- which is all the intended use needs;
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\-\-esp\-listen\-address\f[R] \f[I]ADDR\f[R] widens that, and
\f[CR]0.0.0.0\f[R] means any machine that can reach yours can talk to
the running program.
There is no authentication of any kind in front of it;
.IP \(bu 2
nothing listens until the program asks.
There is no way to open a port from the command line, and no port
survives a Power Reset;
.IP \(bu 2
if the port cannot be bound \- already in use, or an address that is not
yours \- the program is told \f[CR]ERROR\f[R].
jnext never falls back to a different port or a wider address.
.PP
Up to four programs can be connected at once, numbered from 1; number 0
stays reserved for the program\(cqs own outbound connection, so opening
a server never costs it the ability to dial out.
\f[CR]AT+CIPMUX\f[R] cannot be changed while anything is connected.
.PP
A connected client that sends nothing for \f[B]180 seconds\f[R] is hung
up on and the program is told \f[CR]<id>,CLOSED\f[R], which is what a
real ESP\-01 does and the usual reason an idle debug session dies.
\f[CR]AT+CIPSTO=<seconds>\f[R] changes the window (0\-7200, and \f[B]0
means never\f[R]), and \(em again like the real module \(em is refused
with \f[CR]ERROR\f[R] unless a server is already running, so it belongs
\f[I]after\f[R] \f[CR]AT+CIPSERVER=1,<port>\f[R]; the query form
\f[CR]AT+CIPSTO?\f[R] answers either way.
jnext forgets the setting on a reset.
.PP
Still not emulated: TLS and transparent mode (\c
.UR https://github.com/jorgegv/jnext/issues/154
issue #154
.UE \c
).
.SS Making the WiFi go away
A program that has to notice the Next dropping off the network cannot be
tested against a module that is never off it.
Two options schedule an outage, in frames, so a headless run can execute
that code path:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-headless \-\-esp stub.nex \(rs
    \-\-esp\-delayed\-disassociate\-frames 300 \(rs
    \-\-esp\-delayed\-associate\-frames 900 \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames 1200
.EE
.PP
At frame 300 the module loses its association and \f[CR]AT+CIFSR\f[R]
starts reporting \f[CR]+CIFSR:STAIP,\(dq0.0.0.0\(dq\f[R]; at frame 900
it is back, with the same address it had before \- a short outage does
not normally move it, whether the address is static or a DHCP lease that
outlived the gap.
Both edges are reported in the log.
.PP
\f[B]The address report is all that changes.\f[R] Connections already
open keep running, new ones still open, \f[CR]AT+CWJAP?\f[R] and
\f[CR]AT+CIPSTA?\f[R] still answer as they did, and no
\f[CR]WIFI DISCONNECT\f[R] or \f[CR]WIFI CONNECTED\f[R] is sent.
That is a deliberate boundary, not an oversight: what a real module does
with traffic and with unsolicited messages while its access point is
down has not been measured on hardware, and a bench built on an invented
answer would only be measuring the invention.
What \f[I]is\f[R] measured is that a listening program survived a
five\-minute outage on a real Next and never noticed (\c
.UR https://github.com/jorgegv/jnext/issues/246
issue #246
.UE \c
).
.SS Nothing about your host leaks into the program
The module reports a fixed synthetic identity: SSID
\f[CR]JNextWifiHost\f[R], a \f[CR]02:00:00:...\f[R] MAC and BSSID, and a
\f[CR]192.168.1.50\f[R] station address.
None of it is read from your machine, so a program cannot learn your
real SSID, MAC or local addresses by asking.
The emulated module is not a radio and never scans.
.PP
\f[B]\-\-esp\-ip\-address\f[R] changes the station address it claims,
and does not weaken that: the value is one you typed, never one jnext
discovered, and it is still only a string in a reply.
Nothing listens on it and no traffic goes near it.
.SH LOGGING
\f[B]\-\-log\-level\f[R] \f[I]SPEC\f[R] sets per\-subsystem log levels.
A bare level sets every subsystem; a
\f[I]name\f[R]\f[CR]=\f[R]\f[I]level\f[R] pair sets one.
They can be mixed, and are applied left to right:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-log\-level warn                      # everything at warn
jnext \-\-log\-level cpu=trace                 # just the CPU
jnext \-\-log\-level warn,emulator=debug       # quiet, except the emulator
.EE
.PP
Levels are \f[CR]trace\f[R], \f[CR]debug\f[R], \f[CR]info\f[R]
(default), \f[CR]warn\f[R], \f[CR]err\f[R], \f[CR]critical\f[R] and
\f[CR]off\f[R]; \f[CR]warning\f[R], \f[CR]error\f[R], \f[CR]fatal\f[R]
and \f[CR]none\f[R] are accepted as aliases.
.PP
Subsystems are \f[CR]cpu\f[R], \f[CR]memory\f[R], \f[CR]ula\f[R],
\f[CR]video\f[R], \f[CR]audio\f[R], \f[CR]port\f[R], \f[CR]nextreg\f[R],
\f[CR]dma\f[R], \f[CR]copper\f[R], \f[CR]uart\f[R], \f[CR]input\f[R],
\f[CR]platform\f[R], \f[CR]emulator\f[R], \f[CR]sdcard\f[R],
\f[CR]divmmc\f[R], \f[CR]spi\f[R], \f[CR]ctc\f[R], \f[CR]i2c\f[R],
\f[CR]multiface\f[R], \f[CR]esp01\f[R], \f[CR]esxdos\f[R] and
\f[CR]debugger\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]debugger\f[R] is the debugger backend.
At the default \f[CR]info\f[R] level it reports every change a debugger
client makes to the machine \- one \f[CR]MUTATE\f[R] line per register,
memory, NextREG, port, palette or sprite write, naming the old value,
the new one and the client \- plus each client attaching and detaching.
Set it to \f[CR]warn\f[R] to silence that running commentary without
silencing anything else.
.PP
\f[CR]esp01\f[R] is the emulated ESP\-01 WiFi module
(\f[B]\-\-esp\f[R]).
At the default \f[CR]info\f[R] level it reports only the connections
opened, refused, failed and closed \- the events \f[B]NETWORKING\f[R]
describes as never silent.
A refusal or a failure is a warning, so it survives
\f[B]\-\-log\-level\f[R] \f[I]warn\f[R] too.
\f[CR]debug\f[R] adds every AT command received and every response
emitted, and \f[CR]trace\f[R] adds the raw byte traffic in both
directions.
Turning it down to \f[CR]off\f[R] silences the connection reports too,
which is a deliberate choice on the user\(cqs part and not something
jnext undoes.
.PP
\f[CR]esxdos\f[R] is a tracer rather than a subsystem: at
\f[CR]trace\f[R] level every \f[CR]RST $08\f[R] esxdos call is logged
with its arguments and its result, including calls jnext does not
service.
It does not need \f[B]\-\-esxdos\-stub\f[R] \(em the interesting case is
a NextZXOS\-expecting program running without the stub, where those
calls are otherwise invisible.
.SS Sending the log to a file
\f[B]\-\-log\-file\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R] writes the log to \f[I]FILE\f[R]
instead of the console:
.IP
.EX
jnext game.nex \-\-log\-level nextreg=debug \-\-log\-file trace.log
.EE
.PP
It replaces the console rather than adding to it, so the trace is in one
place and nothing is doubled.
\f[I]FILE\f[R] is truncated at the start of every run \- a run\(cqs log
describes that run, and nothing in it belongs to a previous one.
If it cannot be opened, jnext says so and exits non\-zero; it does not
fall back to the console, because a log you asked to be redirected and
silently was not is a trace you lose without noticing.
A missing containing directory is created for you, so
\f[CR]\-\-log\-file logs/run.log\f[R] works in a fresh checkout.
.PP
This exists so that capturing a log does not require getting shell
redirection right.
jnext logs to standard error, so \f[CR]> file\f[R] captures nothing and
\f[CR]2> file\f[R] is needed \- and PowerShell re\-encodes redirected
output from a native program.
\f[B]\-\-log\-file\f[R] sidesteps both: the program writes where it was
told to write.
.PP
A log file carries no colour, whatever \f[CR]NO_COLOR\f[R] says.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[CR]NO_COLOR\f[R]
When set to any non\-empty value, jnext emits no ANSI colour escapes on
its log output, whether or not the console is a terminal \- the
convention described at \c
.UR https://no-color.org/
.UE \c
\&.
An empty value counts as unset, which is how a script re\-enables colour
for one child process.
The value itself is never interpreted: \f[CR]NO_COLOR=0\f[R] suppresses
colour just as \f[CR]NO_COLOR=1\f[R] does, because both are non\-empty.
It has no effect on \f[B]\-\-log\-file\f[R] output, which is never
coloured in the first place.
.SH PROFILING
\f[B]\-\-profile\f[R] enables the CPU T\-state profiler: one histogram
entry per executed instruction, written to \f[B]\-\-profile\-output\f[R]
(default \f[CR]profile.dat\f[R]) on exit.
Join it against a z88dk \f[CR].map\f[R] file to get a per\-function
heatmap:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-headless game.nex \-\-profile \-\-profile\-output game.dat \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit 20
tools/get\-function\-heatmap.pl \-m game.map game.dat
.EE
.SH THE GUI
The Qt6 build gives a native window with menus, a toolbar and a status
bar (FPS, CPU speed, emulator speed, tape status, machine type), Hi\-DPI
pixel\-perfect rendering at integer scale, and a CRT scanline filter.
.PP
Menu shortcuts use \f[B]Alt\f[R], never plain \f[B]Ctrl\f[R]: Ctrl is
the Spectrum\(cqs Symbol Shift, so a Ctrl shortcut would eat a key the
guest needs (see \f[B]THE KEYBOARD\f[R] below).
.TP
\f[B]File\f[R]
Load a program (Alt+O \- NEX/JNS/SNA/SZX/Z80/TAP/TZX/WAV/RZX), Insert SD
Card Image / Eject SD Card (see \f[B]CHANGING THE CARD WHILE
RUNNING\f[R]), Record MPEG4 Video (Ctrl+F5) / Stop (Ctrl+F6), Play RZX /
Record RZX / Stop RZX, Save Screenshot (Alt+S), Quick Screenshot
(Alt+K), Save Snapshot (Alt+Shift+S), Quit (Alt+Q).
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Save Screenshot\&...\f[R] asks for a name; the extension chooses
the format (\f[B]SCREENSHOT FORMATS\f[R] above).
\f[B]Quick Screenshot\f[R] asks nothing: it writes
\f[CR]jnext\-YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS.png\f[R] (or \f[CR].scr\f[R]) into the
quick\-screenshot directory immediately and names the file it wrote in
the status bar, so a sequence of captures costs one keypress each.
The directory and the format are set under \f[B]Settings > Preferences >
Paths\f[R]; the directory defaults to \f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/screenshots\f[R]
and is created on first use.
A name already in use gains a \f[CR]\-02\f[R], \f[CR]\-03\f[R] suffix,
so two captures in the same second cannot overwrite each other.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Machine\f[R]
Power Reset (Alt+R \- power off/on cold boot, full boot chain), Soft
Reset (F4 \- the front\-panel reset button: back to NextZXOS without
re\-running the boot chain; does nothing while the firmware still holds
config mode.
After a direct \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] it resets into 48K BASIC and discards
the loaded program, because that boot never ran the firmware and so has
no NextZXOS to return to \- use Power Reset there), Machine Type (48K /
128K / +3 / Next), CPU Speed (3.5 / 7 / 14 / 28 MHz \- the Next\(cqs own
clock), Emulator Speed (0.5x / 1x / 2x / 4x / custom % \- the host\-side
throttle).
.TP
\f[B]Input\f[R]
Joy 1 Source (port 0x1F) and Joy 2 Source (port 0x37), each selecting
what drives that connector; Capture Mouse, which confines the host
pointer so the Kempston mouse can move freely (Ctrl+Alt releases it).
.TP
\f[B]Tape\f[R]
Open Tape File (Alt+T), Eject, Rewind, Fast Load (toggle).
.TP
\f[B]Debug\f[R]
Magic Breakpoint (toggle), Debugger (Alt+D \- the same entry as in
\f[B]View\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]View\f[R]
Scale 1x / 2x / 3x, Fullscreen (F11, letterboxed), CRT Filter, Debugger
(Alt+D).
.TP
\f[B]Settings\f[R]
Preferences\&...
(Alt+P), which opens the settings dialog (Startup, Input, Audio, Network
and Paths tabs, plus Debugger Keys in a build with the debugger compiled
in).
Its values are saved to the configuration file; command\-line options
always take precedence over them.
.TP
\f[B]Help\f[R]
About.
.PP
The toolbar has Power Reset, Soft Reset, Load, Screenshot, an NMI button
(Multiface NMI) and, in a debugger\-enabled build, a bug button that
opens the debugger.
.PP
CPU Speed and Emulator Speed are different things: the first changes the
clock the emulated Z80 runs at, which is a real Next feature; the second
changes how fast the emulator runs relative to real time.
