.PP
Shift is Caps Shift and Ctrl is Symbol Shift, matching a real Spectrum
Next with a PC keyboard attached.
Earlier releases had the two swapped.
.PP
Holding Shift while pressing a digit or one of the symbol keys above
reaches the guest as Caps Shift plus that key: Shift+1 is Edit, Shift+0
is Delete, Shift+5 to Shift+8 are the four cursor keys, Shift+9 is
Graph, and Shift+\f[CR];\f[R] is Caps Shift + Symbol Shift + O. jnext
keys off the physical key rather than the character your host layout
produces, the same way the Next\(cqs own PS/2 keymap treats the shift
keys as an independent overlay.
Earlier releases dropped all nineteen of those keystrokes before the
Spectrum saw them.
.PP
Esc is the Spectrum\(cqs Break key, not a fullscreen shortcut.
Fullscreen is toggled with F11 only (it used to also be exited with
Esc).
.PP
The function keys are host controls, not Spectrum keys.
F1 is Power Reset (the same as Alt+R), F4 is Soft Reset, F9 fires the
Multiface NMI, F10 fires the DivMMC NMI, F2 cycles the window scale and
F11 toggles fullscreen.
With the debugger open F9 belongs to the debugger (Pause / Break)
instead.
.PP
Ctrl is left alone for the guest.
No Ctrl+letter chord of any kind is a jnext shortcut, so the Symbol
Shift sequences NextBASIC needs constantly \- Ctrl+O \f[CR];\f[R],
Ctrl+D \f[CR]STEP\f[R], Ctrl+R \f[CR]<\f[R], Ctrl+T \f[CR]>\f[R], Ctrl+Q
\f[CR]<=\f[R], Ctrl+S \f[CR]|\f[R] \- all reach the program you are
running, and so does Ctrl+Shift+S (Caps Shift + Symbol Shift + S), which
was the last one to move.
Earlier releases bound those to menu commands and the program never saw
them.
The only Ctrl chords jnext still takes are Ctrl+F5 and Ctrl+F6 (start
and stop video recording), and function keys have no Spectrum meaning to
lose.
.PP
Alt is the opposite: it is a host modifier, never a Spectrum key.
jnext claims Alt + Q/O/S/K/R/T/D/P and Alt+Shift+S (menu shortcuts), Alt
+ F/M/I/A/B/V/N/H (menu bar) and Alt + 1 to Alt + 8 (the script host
keys), which leaves the guest only Alt + E/G/C (EDIT, GRAPH, CAPS LOCK)
and Alt + the key left of \f[CR]1\f[R] (INV VIDEO).
Alt + 1 to Alt + 8 no longer type their digit into the program (earlier
releases let them through as plain 1\-8); Alt + 9 and Alt + 0 still do.
Real Next hardware instead maps Left Alt to EXTEND MODE and Right Alt to
GRAPH; jnext deliberately does not, following the FUSE/ZEsarUX
convention that puts EXTEND MODE on Tab.
.PP
Up to two USB gamepads are picked up automatically (hot\-plug) and
mapped to the Next\(cqs two joystick ports; the joystick mode (Kempston
/ Sinclair / Cursor / MD) follows NextREG 0x05, as on real hardware.
.PP
Either joystick port can instead be driven by the host cursor keys
(arrows for direction, Space for fire), which is useful for the many
games that read the Kempston joystick when you have no gamepad.
Choose the source per connector in the \f[B]Input\f[R] menu, in
\f[B]Settings > Preferences > Input\f[R], or with
\f[B]\-\-joy1\-source\f[R] \f[CR]keys\f[R] / \f[B]\-\-joy2\-source\f[R]
\f[CR]keys\f[R]; the choice is remembered across sessions.
Only one connector may use the cursor keys at a time, and while it does,
the arrows and Space stop acting as ZX keys.
.SH THE DEBUGGER
The debugger opens in its own window (\f[B]Debug > Debugger\f[R] or
\f[B]View > Debugger\f[R] \- the same entry in both menus \- or Alt+D).
It is driven from the Qt6 UI, so it needs a GUI build
(\f[CR]make gui\-release\f[R] or \f[CR]make gui\-debug\f[R]); the
SDL\-only build has no way to open it.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CPU registers\f[R] \- all Z80/Z80N registers, flags
(S/Z/H/P/V/N/C), halt state, interrupt mode, active ULA screen
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]MMU panel\f[R] \- the Next 8\-slot MMU table (page numbers and
type) and the 128K bank mappings
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Disassembly\f[R] \- Z80 + Z80N, PC highlight, breakpoint gutter,
follow\-PC, run\-to\-cursor, symbol names from MAP files.
Lines can be selected (drag, Shift+click, Shift plus the navigation
keys, Ctrl+A) and copied with Ctrl+C as assembly only \- no address
column, no opcode bytes \- or with the right\-click menu\(cqs \f[B]Copy
with Addresses\f[R] to keep both
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Memory hex editor\f[R] \- full 64K, hex and ASCII, inline editing,
page/bank selector
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Stack\f[R] \- SP\-relative word view, SP row highlighted
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Call stack\f[R] \- CALL/RST/INT/RET tracking, with symbol
resolution
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Breakpoints\f[R] \- execution, read, write and I/O watchpoints, in
one panel.
An I/O watchpoint address of \f[B]00\f[R]\-\f[B]FF\f[R] matches any port
with that low byte (\f[B]FE\f[R] catches every ULA access, whatever the
high byte holds); \f[B]0100\f[R] and above matches that exact 16\-bit
port (\f[B]243B\f[R], not \f[B]253B\f[R]).
They are armed only while the debugger window is open; start jnext with
\f[B]\-\-persistent\-breakpoints\f[R] to keep them armed with it closed,
in which case a hit reopens the window at the breakpoint.
Each row has an \f[B]On\f[R] checkbox that suspends that one without
deleting it, and the panel has a \f[B]Breakpoints enabled\f[R] master
switch that suspends every breakpoint and watchpoint at once and
restores each one\(cqs own state when ticked back; a suspended execute
breakpoint is drawn in the gutter as a hollow ring rather than a filled
dot
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Watch expressions\f[R] \- byte, word or long at arbitrary
addresses, with custom labels
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Video panels\f[R] \- All layers (the real composite, through the
live compositor), ULA (primary and shadow), Layer 2 (active and shadow),
Sprites, Tilemap, Background (the NR 0x4A fallback colour);
per\-scanline view up to the current raster position, checkerboard for
transparent pixels.
The header gives the raster position in all four coordinate systems at
once (raw \f[CR]hc\f[R]/\f[CR]vc\f[R], the ULA\(cqs
\f[CR]hc_ula\f[R]/\f[CR]vc_ula\f[R], the Copper \f[CR]cvc\f[R] that NR
0x1E/0x1F report, and the pixel being generated), whether the beam is in
paper, border or blanking, and whether the ULA is fetching a bitmap
byte, an attribute byte or nothing
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Sprite viewer\f[R] \- all 128 hardware sprites with their attribute
table
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Copper disassembly\f[R] \- decoded WAIT/MOVE, with the current
Copper PC
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]NextREG panel\f[R] \- all 256 registers, named, editable inline
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Audio panel\f[R] \- AY register state for the 3 TurboSound chips,
per\-source mute
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Trace log\f[R] \- circular instruction trace buffer, exportable to
a file.
Off by default; enable with \f[B]\-\-trace\f[R], the debugger\(cqs
Enable Trace menu item, or implicitly by enabling rewind, which needs it
for Step Back
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Symbol table\f[R] \- Z88DK MAP files; symbols appear inline in the
disassembly and in watches
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Backwards execution (rewind)\f[R] \- frame\-snapshot ring buffer,
with Step Back, Frame Back and a rewind slider.
Opt\-in, because rewind takes a full\-machine snapshot every frame:
start jnext with \f[B]\-\-rewind\-buffer\-size\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] (for
example 500), or toggle it live from the debugger\(cqs Debug > Rewind >
Enable Rewind menu, with no restart needed \- that allocates 500 frames,
or the last size set via Rewind Buffer Size\&...
Unchecking the toggle pauses snapshotting but keeps the recorded
history; set the buffer size to 0 to free the memory
.PP
