.PP
Those are defaults.
All twelve debugger commands \- the nine above plus Run to Cursor, Run
to End of Frame and Run to End of Scan Line, which ship unbound \- can
be rebound under \f[B]Settings > Preferences > Debugger Keys\f[R] in the
emulator window, or by hand in the \f[CR][debugger_keys]\f[R] section of
the configuration file (\f[CR]step_over=F10\f[R]).
Only the commands you change are written there, so a default that
changes in a later release still reaches you.
.PP
The window may be resized below what the panels need: the panel area
then scrolls rather than clipping, and the control toolbar along the
bottom stays visible, with any buttons that no longer fit reachable from
its overflow menu.
Its size is remembered between sessions and clamped to the screen it
reopens on.
.SH REMOTE DEBUGGING (DEZOG)
With \f[B]\-\-dzrp\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R], jnext serves the DeZog
Remote Protocol (DZRP, version 2.2.0), the protocol DeZog \- the Z80
debugger for Visual Studio Code \- uses to drive CSpect and real
hardware.
DeZog then debugs the program running in jnext from your editor:
source\-level breakpoints, stepping, registers, memory, the MMU slots,
NextREGs and sprites.
.PP
In DeZog\(cqs \f[CR]launch.json\f[R], point a \f[CR]cspect\f[R] remote
at jnext\(cqs port:
.IP
.EX
\(dqremoteType\(dq: \(dqcspect\(dq,
\(dqcspect\(dq: { \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqlocalhost\(dq, \(dqport\(dq: 11000 }
.EE
.PP
and start jnext with the same port, for example
\f[CR]jnext \-\-dzrp\-port 11000 \-\-load mygame.nex\f[R].
DeZog releases that have a \f[CR]dzrp\f[R] remote type can use it
instead, with the same port.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Connecting pauses the machine\f[R], and DeZog shows where it
stopped.
Disconnecting resumes it if the pause was DeZog\(cqs own and no other
client \- jnext\(cqs open debugger window, another remote debugger \- is
attached; if one is, the machine stays paused and the pause becomes that
client\(cqs.
A pause made from jnext\(cqs debugger window stays.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Breakpoints and watchpoints set from DeZog belong to that
session\f[R]: they are separate from the ones set in jnext\(cqs debugger
window, they stop the machine whether that window is open or not, and
they are removed when DeZog disconnects \- a DeZog that crashes cannot
leave the machine stopped on them.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Either side may pause or resume.\f[R] jnext\(cqs debugger window
and DeZog drive the same machine and whoever acted last wins.
When something other than DeZog stops the machine while DeZog has it
running \- the debugger window, a magic breakpoint \- DeZog is told it
stopped, and why.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Saving and restoring the state\f[R] (DeZog\(cqs
\f[CR]\-state save\f[R] / \f[CR]\-state restore\f[R]) keeps the state
inside jnext for this session only; DeZog\(cqs file holds a reference to
it.
A state can only be saved at a frame boundary, so after a breakpoint hit
the save is refused \- pause the machine by hand first.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Not served\f[R]: the commands a hardware stub needs to patch
breakpoints into memory (\f[CR]CMD_SET_BREAKPOINTS\f[R],
\f[CR]CMD_RESTORE_MEM\f[R]) and \f[CR]CMD_EXEC_ASM\f[R].
They are answered empty, with a warning in the log, so the client never
waits.
.PP
In \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] mode a client that holds the machine paused
holds its frames too: nothing is emulated and jnext waits for the next
command without using the CPU.
The automatic exit (\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] and its
\f[B]\-frames\f[R] form) is still a hard bound: while a client holds the
machine paused it is counted in wall time, one frame per 20 ms.
.PP
The server listens on \f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R] unless
\f[B]\-\-debug\-listen\-address\f[R] says otherwise.
DZRP has no authentication: anyone who can reach the port controls the
machine and can read and write all of its memory.
.SH REMOTE DEBUGGING (Z88DK\-GDB)
With \f[B]\-\-gdb\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R], jnext serves the GDB remote
serial protocol to \f[CR]z88dk\-gdb\f[R], z88dk\(cqs command\-line
debugger \- a distribution\(cqs own gdb has no Z80 support.
Build the program with a map file (\f[CR]\-m\f[R] on the \f[CR]zcc\f[R]
line) and connect to jnext\(cqs port:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-gdb\-port 3333 \-\-load mygame.nex
z88dk\-gdb \-h 127.0.0.1 \-p 3333 \-x mygame.map
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Connecting stops the machine\f[R], and \f[CR]quit\f[R] resumes it
if the stop was the client\(cqs own and no other client (jnext\(cqs open
debugger window, another remote debugger) is attached \- if one is, the
machine stays paused and the pause becomes that client\(cqs; a pause
made from jnext\(cqs debugger window stays.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Served\f[R]: registers (AF BC DE HL, the alternate set, IX IY SP
PC, and a T\-state clock the client\(cqs profiler reads), memory as the
Z80 sees it now (the 64 KB CPU view \- a write that touches ROM is
refused), breakpoints, \f[CR]stepi\f[R], \f[CR]nexti\f[R],
\f[CR]cont\f[R], Ctrl\-C and \f[CR]quit\f[R].
Breakpoints belong to the session: they stop the machine whether
jnext\(cqs debugger window is open or not, and they are removed when the
client disconnects.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]I, R, IFF1/IFF2 and IM are not in the register set\f[R] on purpose:
the client\(cqs \f[CR]set\f[R] writes the whole set at once, and zeroes
every register it does not know.
Read and write them with \f[CR]monitor regs\f[R] and
\f[CR]monitor set\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB]monitor\f[B] commands\f[R] (z88dk\-gdb versions that have
\f[CR]monitor\f[R]) reach the Next\-specific state: \f[CR]mmu\f[R],
\f[CR]nextreg\f[R], \f[CR]page\f[R] (a physical 8 KB page),
\f[CR]in\f[R]/\f[CR]out\f[R] (a real port access, which can change the
device\(cqs state), \f[CR]sym\f[R], \f[CR]time\f[R],
\f[CR]reset soft\f[R]/\f[CR]reset hard\f[R] and \f[CR]bp\f[R].
\f[CR]monitor help\f[R] lists them.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Either side may pause or resume\f[R], as with DeZog: the client is
told the machine stopped when something else stops it while the client
has it running.
.PP
In \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] mode a client holding the machine stopped
holds its frames too, exactly as described for DeZog above.
The GDB protocol has no authentication either.
.SH SCRIPTING
A debugger script (\f[CR].jds\f[R], loaded with \f[B]\-\-script\f[R]) is
a list of rules, each an event and the actions to run when it happens:
.IP
.EX
# palette.jds \- CI assertion: the exit status is the verdict
on execute \(atpalette_init_done once do
    assert mem[0x9000] == 0xAA \(dqsentinel missing in palette buffer\(dq
    log \(dqPASS palette init\(dq
    exit 0
end
on write 0x4000..0x5AFF when VALUE == 0xB7 do
    stop \(dqforbidden value ${VALUE:x2} written by ${PC:x4}\(dq
end
.EE
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-headless \-\-map game.map \-\-script palette.jds \-\-load game.nex \(rs
      \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames 600
.EE
.PP
The events are an instruction about to execute, a memory read or write,
a port read or write, a NextREG write, a frame, a scanline, a
master\-clock cycle, an accepted interrupt, an NMI, a reset, a host key,
a Copper \f[CR]MOVE\f[R]/\f[CR]WAIT\f[R]/\f[CR]HALT\f[R], a DMA
transfer\(cqs start, bytes and end, and any pause of the machine.
A rule\(cqs \f[CR]when\f[R] condition is checked by the debugger itself,
so a rule body runs only for the accesses that match.
A script observes the machine without disturbing it; it changes the
machine only through \f[CR]set\f[R] and \f[CR]out\f[R], and each such
write is logged as a \f[CR]MUTATE\f[R] line.
Script output goes to the log as \f[CR][jds F:\f[R]\f[I]frame\f[R]
\f[CR]C:\f[R]\f[I]cycle\f[R]\f[CR]]\f[R] lines.
.PP
\f[B]Host keys.\f[R] In the emulator window (Qt or SDL) and in the
debugger window, Alt + 1 to Alt + 8 are the script host keys: Alt +
\f[I]N\f[R] runs the loaded scripts\(cq \f[CR]on hostkey\f[R]
\f[I]N\f[R] rules, with or without the debugger window open.
The digit never reaches the guest, even when no script is loaded, so
what Alt + 1 does never depends on what is loaded.
\f[B]\-\-script\-key\f[R] is the same key in \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R].
A debugger key binding can never use them.
.PP
\f[B]The Script tab.\f[R] In the Qt GUI the debugger window\(cqs Script
tab lists the loaded scripts\(cq rules (the event with its filter, its
state, its hit count), the run\(cqs verdict (a stop or failed assert, an
\f[CR]exit\f[R], a run\-time error, the verdicts not reached yet) and
the script log.
\f[B]Script > Load Script\&...\f[R] loads one more script while the
machine runs, \f[B]Reload Scripts\f[R] loads the same files again and
\f[B]Unload Scripts\f[R] removes them all; scripts given with
\f[B]\-\-script\f[R] appear there too.
A script loaded from the menu registers at once, and \f[CR]FRAME\f[R]
(and \f[CR]on frame\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]) count the machine\(cqs frames, not
frames since the load.
In the GUI a script never ends the program: \f[CR]stop\f[R], a failed
\f[CR]assert\f[R] and \f[CR]exit\f[R] pause the machine and open the
debugger.
.PP
\f[B]Keys.\f[R] \f[CR]press\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R] holds a key down until
\f[CR]release\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R] (\f[CR]press\f[R] \f[I]KEY\f[R]
\f[CR]for\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] is a pulse of \f[I]N\f[R] frames).
\f[I]KEY\f[R] is a name \f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\f[R] takes, a single
matrix position \f[CR]row,col\f[R] (\f[CR]0,0\f[R] is CAPS SHIFT,
\f[CR]7,1\f[R] SYMBOL SHIFT), or one of the Next\(cqs extended keys as
\f[CR]ext:\f[R]\f[I]name\f[R] \- \f[CR]ext:right\f[R],
\f[CR]ext:left\f[R], \f[CR]ext:down\f[R], \f[CR]ext:up\f[R],
\f[CR]ext:dot\f[R], \f[CR]ext:comma\f[R], \f[CR]ext:quote\f[R],
\f[CR]ext:semicolon\f[R], \f[CR]ext:extend\f[R],
\f[CR]ext:capslock\f[R], \f[CR]ext:graph\f[R], \f[CR]ext:truevideo\f[R],
\f[CR]ext:invvideo\f[R], \f[CR]ext:break\f[R], \f[CR]ext:edit\f[R],
\f[CR]ext:delete\f[R] (the keys NR 0xB0 / 0xB1 read; the PC\(cqs arrows,
Backspace and Esc are among them).
An extended key has no \f[CR]for\f[R] pulse.
.PP
\f[B]Recording a session.\f[R] \f[B]Script > Record Script\&...\f[R] in
the debugger window (or the Script tab\(cqs \f[B]Record\&...\f[R], or
\f[B]\-\-record\-script\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]) records what you do into a
replay script: every change of the keyboard, the Next extended keys and
both joysticks, as \f[CR]press\f[R] / \f[CR]release\f[R] /
\f[CR]joystick\f[R] at the frame it landed on, and a capture at each
\f[B]Alt + 8\f[R] (or \f[B]Script > Capture Screen\f[R]).
A capture is the ULA screen memory (\f[I]base\f[R]\f[CR]\-0001.scr\f[R]
and a \f[CR]compare_scr\f[R] line) when only the ULA layer is on, and
otherwise a PNG (\f[I]base\f[R]\f[CR]\-0001.png\f[R], and a
\f[CR]screenshot\f[R] of \f[I]base\f[R]\f[CR]\-0001\-replay.png\f[R] to
compare it with).
\f[B]Stop Recording\f[R] \- or leaving jnext \- writes the script.
Its header says what it was recorded on and the command that replays it:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-headless \-\-machine next \-\-load game.nex \-\-script session.jds \(rs
      \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames 1010
.EE
.PP
run in the directory holding the captures (the script names them
relative to it).
The replay asserts the machine type and the joystick mode (NR 0x05) it
was recorded with, applies the same input at the same frames, and ends
with \f[CR]exit 0\f[R]: a \f[CR].scr\f[R] that differs exits
\f[CR]3\f[R].
A cold boot (a hard reset, or loading a program from the menu) restarts
the recording, because a replay starts at power\-on too.
What cannot be replayed exactly is written into the script as a
\f[CR]WARNING\f[R] (and logged again when it replays): a key changed
while the machine was paused in the middle of a frame, input held before
the recording began, a rewind.
Keys jnext types by itself (a tape\(cqs \f[CR]LOAD \(dq\(dq\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-keypress\f[R]) are recorded like any others, so replay
without the options that typed them.
.PP
\f[B]Exit status\f[R] in \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] (and the SDL\-only
build): \f[CR]0\f[R] for a clean run or \f[CR]exit 0\f[R]; \f[I]N\f[R]
for \f[CR]exit\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] (0 to 255: any other value is a
run\-time error); \f[CR]3\f[R] for a \f[CR]stop\f[R] or a failed
\f[CR]assert\f[R]; \f[CR]1\f[R] for a script that does not load or a
run\-time error (division by zero, say \- the rule is disabled and the
run ends at the next frame).
The first status a run reaches is kept.
When \f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames\f[R] ends a run before a
script reached a verdict it declared \- a rule holding \f[CR]exit\f[R]
or \f[CR]compare_scr\f[R] that never ran, or a \f[B]\-\-script\-key\f[R]
not yet delivered \- the run exits \f[CR]3\f[R]
(\f[CR]SCRIPT: N deferred actions never ran\f[R]).
A status \f[CR]2\f[R] never comes from a script.
A failure wins over a success at the same moment: when a
\f[CR]stop\f[R], a failed \f[CR]assert\f[R] or \f[CR]compare_scr\f[R],
or a run\-time error happens at the same event as an \f[CR]exit 0\f[R]
\- in the same rule or in another one, before it or after it \- the run
exits \f[CR]3\f[R] (or \f[CR]1\f[R] for the run\-time error), and the
log says \f[CR]SCRIPT EXIT 0 not taken\f[R].
A non\-zero \f[CR]exit\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R] is kept.
An \f[CR]exit\f[R] in an \f[CR]on stop\f[R] rule never changes the
status: the pause that ran it already decided it (a stop\(cqs
\f[CR]3\f[R]).
An \f[CR]exit\f[R] that comes while a \f[CR]compare_scr\f[R] is still
waiting for its frame edge waits with it, so \f[CR]on write\f[R]
\f[I]A\f[R] \f[CR]do compare_scr\f[R] \f[I]F\f[R] \f[I]M\f[R]
\f[CR]exit 0 end\f[R] reports a mismatch rather than exiting first.
.PP
The user guide\(cqs \f[B]Debugger scripting\f[R] chapter is the full
reference: every event with its payload, every action, the state names,
and worked examples.
.SH REMOTE DEBUGGING (ZRCP)
With \f[B]\-\-zrcp\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R], jnext serves the text
protocol ZEsarUX offers on its \f[CR]\-\-remoteprotocol\-port\f[R]
(ZRCP), speaking as ZEsarUX 12.0 does: a welcome, then one command per
line, each answered by its reply and a prompt \- \f[CR]command>\f[R], or
\f[CR]command\(atcpu\-step>\f[R] while the machine is held in cpu\-step
mode.
Try it by hand with \f[CR]telnet localhost PORT\f[R] and
\f[CR]help\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Served\f[R]: the session and information commands
(\f[CR]about\f[R], \f[CR]get\-version\f[R],
\f[CR]get\-current\-machine\f[R], \f[CR]help\f[R], \f[CR]ls\f[R],
\f[CR]set\-cr\f[R], \f[CR]quit\f[R] \&...), inspection
(\f[CR]get\-registers\f[R], \f[CR]set\-register\f[R],
\f[CR]read\-memory\f[R], \f[CR]write\-memory\f[R], \f[CR]hexdump\f[R],
\f[CR]disassemble\f[R], \f[CR]get\-memory\-pages\f[R],
\f[CR]get\-tstates\f[R], \f[CR]evaluate\f[R], the \f[CR]tbblue\-*\f[R]
registers, sprites, patterns, palettes and clip windows), control
(\f[CR]enter\-cpu\-step\f[R], \f[CR]cpu\-step\f[R],
\f[CR]cpu\-step\-over\f[R], \f[CR]run\f[R], \f[CR]run\f[R] \f[I]n\f[R],
\f[CR]hard\-reset\-cpu\f[R], \f[CR]reset\-cpu\f[R],
\f[CR]generate\-nmi\f[R]) and breakpoints (the 100 condition slots of
\f[CR]set\-breakpoint\f[R], \f[CR]set\-breakpointaction\f[R],
\f[CR]enable\-breakpoint\f[R], \f[CR]disable\-breakpoint\f[R],
\f[CR]enable\-breakpoints\f[R], \f[CR]disable\-breakpoints\f[R],
\f[CR]get\-breakpoints\f[R], and the memory breakpoints of
\f[CR]set\-membreakpoint\f[R], \f[CR]get\-membreakpoints\f[R],
\f[CR]clear\-membreakpoints\f[R]), and history, coverage and loading
(\f[CR]cpu\-history\f[R], \f[CR]cpu\-code\-coverage\f[R],
\f[CR]extended\-stack\f[R], \f[CR]smartload\f[R],
\f[CR]load\-binary\f[R], \f[CR]save\-binary\f[R],
\f[CR]snapshot\-save\f[R], \f[CR]snapshot\-load\f[R]).
\f[CR]help\f[R] \f[I]command\f[R] describes each one, with the
divergences from ZEsarUX that a client can see in its replies.
Numbers are decimal or hexadecimal with an \f[CR]H\f[R] suffix
(\f[CR]8000H\f[R]); a malformed or out\-of\-range value is refused
rather than read as something else.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB]run\f[B] stops on any data sent\f[R]: while it runs, any line
the client sends stops the machine and is discarded, not executed.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB]hard\-reset\-cpu\f[B] is a cold boot\f[R] of the whole
machine, complete before the reply; a paused machine stays paused.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Breakpoint conditions are ZEsarUX\(cqs\f[R] (\f[CR]PC=8000H\f[R],
\f[CR]SP>=65280\f[R], \f[CR]PC=PEEKW(SP\-2) AND SP>=65280\f[R],
\f[CR]A<>0 AND (HL&FFH)=5\f[R] \&...), grouped the way ZEsarUX groups
them; \f[CR]help set\-breakpoint\f[R] lists what is honoured and what is
declined.
A condition with no \f[CR]PC=\f[R] first term fires only when it turns
from false to true, as in ZEsarUX: one that stays true stops (or prints)
once.
A \f[CR]PC=\f[R] breakpoint fires at every arrival at its address,
except at the address a run resumes from.
Breakpoints belong to the session: they start disabled, as in ZEsarUX,
and are removed when the client disconnects.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]History, coverage and call tracking are the machine\(cqs\f[R]: a
client turns off only what it turned on, and its
\f[CR]cpu\-history clear\f[R] / \f[CR]set\-max\-size\f[R] change only
its own view, never the trace jnext\(cqs Step Back reads.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB]snapshot\-save\f[B] / \f[CB]snapshot\-load\f[B] are
in\-memory snapshots of the session\f[R] (nothing is written to disk),
saved with the machine paused while running, not at a breakpoint.
\f[B]\f[CB]smartload\f[B], \f[CB]load\-binary\f[B] and
\f[CB]save\-binary\f[B] read and write files on the host running
jnext\f[R], as ZEsarUX\(cqs do.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Declined\f[R]: \f[CR]exit\-emulator\f[R], and the \f[CR]run\f[R]
options \f[CR]verbose\f[R], \f[CR]no\-stop\-on\-data\f[R] and
\f[CR]update\-immediately\f[R].
ZEsarUX commands jnext does not serve answer
\f[CR]Error. Unsupported command in jnext:\f[R] and the name.
.PP
Connecting does not pause the machine; \f[CR]enter\-cpu\-step\f[R] does.
Disconnecting (or \f[CR]quit\f[R]) resumes it if the pause was the
client\(cqs own and no other client is attached; if one is, the machine
stays paused and the pause becomes that client\(cqs.
One client at a time: a second connection is answered
\f[CR]Error. Another ZRCP client is connected\f[R] and closed.
In \f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] mode a client that holds the machine paused
holds its frames too, exactly as for DZRP.
ZRCP has no authentication either: the server listens on
\f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R] unless \f[B]\-\-debug\-listen\-address\f[R] says
otherwise.
.PP
DeZog connects with its \f[CR]zrcp\f[R] remote
(\f[CR]\(dqremoteType\(dq: \(dqzrcp\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dqzrcp\(dq: {\(dqport\(dq: PORT}\f[R] in
\f[CR]launch.json\f[R]).
Its default \f[CR]resetOnLaunch: true\f[R] starts each session with
\f[CR]hard\-reset\-cpu\f[R], the cold boot above; keep
\f[CR]skipInterrupt\f[R] at its default \f[CR]false\f[R], since jnext
does not step over interrupt routines (\f[CR]set\-debug\-settings\f[R]
bit 5 is refused, and DeZog shows that once as a warning).
.SH MAGIC BREAKPOINT AND MAGIC PORT
The magic breakpoint uses the \f[CR]ED FF\f[R] (ZEsarUX) and
\f[CR]DD 01\f[R] (CSpect) opcodes to pause the debugger when enabled;
execution then resumes right after the two bytes.
When disabled, and on real hardware, \f[CR]ED FF\f[R] is a two\-byte
NOP, but \f[CR]DD 01\f[R] is not: the \f[CR]DD\f[R] prefix is ignored
and \f[CR]01 nn nn\f[R] executes as \f[CR]LD BC,nn\f[R], consuming the
two bytes that follow and overwriting \f[CR]BC\f[R].
Enable it with \f[B]\-\-magic\-breakpoint\f[R] or \f[B]Debug > Magic
Breakpoint\f[R].
.PP
The magic debug port logs writes to a configurable port to stderr as
hex, decimal, ASCII or line\-buffered text.
Enable it with \f[B]\-\-magic\-port\f[R] \f[I]PORT\f[R] and
\f[B]\-\-magic\-port\-mode\f[R] \f[I]MODE\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Boot NextZXOS / the TBBlue firmware (the default machine is
\f[CR]next\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
jnext
.EE
.PP
The same, with an explicit SD\-card image:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-sdcard \(ti/.jnext/sdcard/cspect\-next\-1gb\-fixed.img
.EE
.PP
Run a program; a bare filename is shorthand for \f[B]\-\-load\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
jnext game.nex
jnext game.tap          # auto\-types LOAD \(dq\(dq
jnext game.sna
.EE
.PP
Run as a plain ZX Spectrum 48K (the 48K BASIC ROM comes from the SD
image):
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-machine 48k
.EE
.PP
Run at double speed:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-speed 200
.EE
.PP
Inject a raw binary at 0x8000 and run it:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-inject program.bin \-\-inject\-org 8000
.EE
.PP
Take a headless screenshot, for CI.
Name the SD image explicitly: with no \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] and no cached
image, provisioning prompts on stdin and declines at EOF, so an
unattended run would exit non\-zero instead of capturing anything
(\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-download\-confirm\f[R] is the alternative).
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-headless \-\-machine 48k \(rs
    \-\-sdcard \(ti/.jnext/sdcard/cspect\-next\-1gb\-fixed.img \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-screenshot /tmp/test.png \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-screenshot\-frames 200 \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit 5
.EE
.PP
Capture Layer 2 on its own, then the ULA and sprites together:
.IP
.EX
jnext \-\-headless game.nex \-\-delayed\-screenshot l2.png \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-screenshot\-layers layer2 \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-screenshot\-frames 200 \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames 250
jnext \-\-headless game.nex \-\-delayed\-screenshot us.png \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-screenshot\-layers ula,sprites \(rs
    \-\-delayed\-screenshot\-frames 200 \-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\-frames 250
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/jnext.conf\f[R]
GUI configuration, in INI format.
Written by \f[B]Settings > Preferences\f[R].
Host gains are stored under \f[CR][audio]\f[R] as \f[CR]gain_db\f[R]
(master), \f[CR]gain_beeper_db\f[R], \f[CR]gain_ay0_db\f[R],
\f[CR]gain_ay1_db\f[R], \f[CR]gain_ay2_db\f[R], and
\f[CR]gain_dac_db\f[R].
The degradation policy is stored under \f[CR][startup]\f[R] as
\f[CR]when_slow_prefer\f[R] (\f[CR]audio\f[R] or \f[CR]video\f[R], the
persistent form of \f[B]\-\-when\-slow\-prefer\f[R]); an unrecognised
value keeps the default rather than changing how the emulator degrades.
The emulated ESP\-01 is stored under \f[CR][esp]\f[R] as
\f[CR]enabled\f[R] (\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R], the persistent
form of \f[B]\-\-esp\f[R]) and \f[CR]allowed_hosts\f[R] (a
comma\-separated list, the persistent form of \f[B]\-\-esp\-allow\f[R]),
both edited under \f[B]Settings > Preferences > Network\f[R].
Whenever \f[CR]enabled\f[R] is set the status bar carries the ESP cell
\f[B]NETWORKING\f[R] describes, so neither a config file nor that page
can put the guest on the network without saying so.
\f[B]\-\-no\-esp\f[R] overrides it for one run.
The quick screenshot is stored under \f[CR][screenshot]\f[R] as
\f[CR]quick_dir\f[R] (empty means \f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/screenshots\f[R])
and \f[CR]quick_format\f[R] (\f[CR]png\f[R] or \f[CR]scr\f[R]), edited
under \f[B]Settings > Preferences > Paths\f[R]; an unrecognised format
keeps the default.
CLI options always take precedence over saved values, and headless runs
never read it.
.TP
\f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/screenshots/\f[R]
Where \f[B]File > Quick Screenshot\f[R] writes, unless
\f[CR][screenshot] quick_dir\f[R] says otherwise.
Created on the first capture.
.TP
\f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/warm\-start/\f[R]
The recorded NextZXOS\-resident machine a \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] of a
\f[CR].nex\f[R] starts from, one file per machine type, around 100 KB
each.
Written on the first such load with a given SD image and re\-recorded
whenever the image, the machine type or the state format changes \- see
\f[B]\-\-warm\-start\-regenerate\f[R].
Deleting it costs one cold boot, nothing else.
.TP
\f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/sdcard/cspect\-next\-1gb\-fixed.img\f[R]
The default SD\-card image, used when \f[B]\-\-sdcard\f[R] is not given.
.TP
\f[CR]\(ti/.jnext/sdcard/cspect\-next\-1gb.img\f[R]
The canonical distribution image the patched one above is produced from.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\f[B]jnext\f[R] exits 0 on success and non\-zero on error.
In particular it exits non\-zero when a
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-screenshot\f[R] was requested but never taken, rather
than silently writing nothing, and likewise when a \f[B]\-\-record\f[R]
recording fails to materialize as a usable output file, and when
\f[B]\-\-delayed\-automatic\-exit\f[R] fires before other deferred work
(a \f[B]\-\-load\f[R], an \f[B]\-\-inject\f[R], a keypress\&...)
has happened \(em see that option.
.PP
It also exits non\-zero when the program given to \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] (or
as a bare file name) fails to load, whatever its format, including an
RZX recording given to \f[B]\-\-load\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-rzx\-play\f[R].
This holds in the GUI, the SDL\-only build and under
\f[B]\-\-headless\f[R] alike.
The error is logged, the machine keeps running without the program, and
the non\-zero status is reported when \f[B]jnext\f[R] exits.
.PP
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-add\f[R] is a scriptable non\-emulation mode, so
it reports which failure happened rather than a single non\-zero status:
.TP
0
The file or directory was copied onto the card.
.TP
1
Command\-line usage error \(em \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-dest\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-force\f[R] without
\f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-add\f[R], either of the first two given twice or
with an empty value, or combined with \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-readonly\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-load\f[R].
.TP
2
The host file is missing, unreadable, shrank while being copied, or is 4
GiB or larger (which FAT32 cannot store); or, inside a directory, a
device, FIFO or socket, an unreadable directory, a dangling symbolic
link, or one that loops back.
.TP
3
The destination path is unusable: malformed or non\-ASCII, a name FAT
forbids, a component that already exists as a file, or a destination
that already exists as a directory (for a file) or as a file (for a
directory).
For a directory this includes a host name inside it that FAT cannot
hold, and two names that differ only in case.
.TP
4
A destination file already exists and \f[B]\-\-sdcard\-file\-force\f[R]
was not given.
Nothing was written.
.TP
5
The SD image is missing, is not an MBR\-partitioned FAT32 image, holds a
filesystem this build will not mount (an under\-clustered one), or could
not be written.
.TP
6
The card has no room \(em not enough free space, or a directory with no
free slots.
